hadiths[447]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4324","'Ali رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained what Allah has imposed upon the rich Muslims for the sake of their poor, and the severe reckoning for those who waste it. > **""Indeed Allah has obligated upon the rich Muslims, in their wealth, an amount sufficient for their poor. The poor will never suffer hardship when hungry and unclothed except through what their rich withhold. Verily Allah will call them to a severe reckoning and punish them with a painful punishment.""**"
"4325","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the complaint of the poor against the rich on the Day of Resurrection, Allah's response, and then recited the verse of the known right for the beggar and the deprived. > **""Woe to the rich from the poor on the Day of Resurrection. They will say: Our Lord, they wronged us concerning our rights which You obligated for us upon them. So Allah the Exalted will say: By My Might and Majesty, I will surely bring you near and distance them.""**"
"4326","'Alqamah رضي الله عنه reports that he with others came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ told them what completes their Islam. In the wording of al-Kabir it is ""part of the perfection."" > **""Indeed the perfection of your Islam is that you pay the zakat of your wealth.""**"
"4327","Abu al-Darda' رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a short statement describing the position of zakat in Islam. > **""Zakat is the bridge of Islam.""**"
"4328","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that Islam consists of eight shares, naming each, and the loss of whoever has no share. > **""Islam is eight shares: Islam is a share, prayer is a share, fasting is a share, zakat is a share, pilgrimage to the House is a share, enjoining right is a share, forbidding wrong is a share, and striving in the path of Allah is a share. Ruined is whoever has no share.""**"
"4329","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar that they were commanded to establish prayer and pay zakat, and that prayer is not accepted from one who refuses zakat."
"4330","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirm that zakat is due in each of these kinds of wealth. > **""In camels is their sadaqah, in sheep is their sadaqah, in cattle is their sadaqah, and in wheat is its sadaqah.""**"
"4331","'Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered him to bring a tray for writing what would prevent the ummah from straying after him, so he feared death would intervene, said he would memorize and retain it, and the Prophet ﷺ gave his bequest. > **""I enjoin prayer and zakat, and those whom your right hands possess.""**"
"4332","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that a man from Banu Tamim with abundant wealth, family, property and settled life came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking how to act and spend, the Prophet ﷺ answered him, the man asked for brevity, the Prophet ﷺ summarized, and the man asked whether payment to the Prophet's messenger absolves him before Allah and His Messenger. > **""Pay the zakat of your wealth, for it is a purification that will purify you. Join your relatives, and recognize the right of the poor, the neighbor, and the beggar. Give the near relative his right, and the poor, and the wayfarer, and do not squander wastefully. Yes, when you pay it to my messenger you are absolved of it, its reward is yours, and its sin is upon whoever alters it.""**"
"4333","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning people who accept Islam that they retain what they possessed, with only sadaqah due upon it. In the wording of al-Bazzar and al-Tabarani it concerns the people of the covenant. > **""They shall keep what they accepted Islam upon of their land, their attendants, and their livestock, and upon them concerning it is only sadaqah.""**"
"4334","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that a man from the people asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about one who pays the zakat of his wealth, and the Prophet ﷺ answered him. > **""Whoever pays the zakat of his wealth, its evil has departed from him.""**"
"4335","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports a hadith he heard from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, though he himself had not heard it directly despite his close attendance upon the Prophet ﷺ. > **""No wealth is destroyed on land or sea except by withholding zakat.""**"
"4336","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded guarding wealth, treating the sick, and preparing for affliction. > **""Fortify your wealth with zakat, treat your sick with sadaqah, and prepare supplication for affliction.""**"
"4337","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the abode on the Day of Resurrection of one who refuses zakat. > **""The withholder of zakat on the Day of Resurrection will be in the Fire.""**"
"4338","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defined what is and is not kanz, even if buried beneath seven earths or left in the open. > **""Every wealth, even if beneath seven earths, whose zakat is paid is not kanz. And every wealth whose zakat is not paid, even if in the open, is kanz.""**"
"4339","Abu Shaddad, a man of the people of Oman, reports that the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them commanding testimony, zakat, and marking mosques, with warning of battle, and that no reader was found until a boy read it to them. 'Abd al-'Aziz asked who ruled Oman then, and he said Siwar, one of Kisra's officers. > **""As for what follows: affirm the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah, pay zakat, and mark out the mosques in such and such manner, otherwise I will raid you.""**"
"4340","Thawban رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what will confront one who leaves a hoard behind him on the Day of Resurrection, its questioning, its answer, and its seizing of him. > **""Whoever leaves a hoard behind him, it will be likened for him on the Day of Resurrection to a bald serpent with two spots, following him. He will say: Woe to you, what are you? It will say: I am your treasure which you hoarded. It will not leave him until it takes his hand into its mouth, then follows with the rest of his body.""**"
"4341","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that unpaid sadaqah — or he said zakat, the narrator doubting — destroys the wealth it mixes with. > **""Sadaqah — or he said: zakat — never mixes with wealth except that it corrupts it.""**"
"4342","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described hypocrites for whom prayer was visible but zakat was hidden. > **""Prayer was visible to them so they prayed it, and zakat was hidden from them so they devoured it. They are the hypocrites.""**"
"4343","Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that no camel owner who fails to pay their due in hardship and ease will escape being spread out for them on a flat plain on the Day of Resurrection until judgment is passed. > **""There is no owner of camels who does not pay their right in their hardship and ease except that he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and spread out for them on a flat plain, and they will trample him with their hooves. Whenever the first of them passes, the last returns upon him, until judgment is passed among the people and he sees his path.""**"
"4344","Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the coming of camels, cattle without exemption for hornless or broken-horned, sheep goring and trampling, and the hoard as a bald serpent placing the man's hand in its mouth, for those who did not pay their due. > **""There is no owner of camels who did not pay their due except that they will be brought on the Day of Resurrection and walk over him on a plain, trampling him with their hooves. And the owner of cattle who did not pay their due will be brought and they will walk over him, trampling him with their cloven hooves, with none hornless nor broken-horned among them. And the owner of sheep who did not pay their due will be brought and they will walk over him on a plain, butting him with their horns and trampling him with their hooves, with none hornless nor broken-horned among them. And the owner of treasure will be brought and it will be likened to a bald serpent, and he will find nothing, so it will put his hand into its mouth.""**"
"4345","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ link faith in Allah and His Messenger and the Last Day to three duties. > **""Whoever believes in Allah and His Messenger, let him pay the zakat of his wealth. Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak truth or remain silent. Whoever believes in Allah and His Messenger, let him honor his guest.""**"
"4346","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said five things come with five, and when asked what that meant he explained each breach and its consequence for covenant, judgment, indecency, zakat, and measure. > **""Five are with five. No people break the covenant except that their enemy is given power over them. They do not judge by other than what Allah revealed except that poverty spreads among them. Indecency does not appear among them except that death spreads among them. They do not withhold zakat except that rain is withheld from them. They do not cheat the measure except that growth is withheld from them and they are seized by years of famine.""**"
"4347","'Abdullah ibn 'Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold a time when hearts resemble those of non-Arabs, explained it as love of the world, with Bedouin ways, wealth spent on livestock, jihad seen as loss and zakat as a penalty. > **""There will surely come upon people a time when their hearts are the hearts of non-Arabs: love of the world. Their way will be the way of the Bedouins. Whatever provision comes to them they will put into livestock. They will regard jihad as harm and zakat as a fine.""**"
"4348","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar that withholding zakat corrupts lawful earnings, while zakat does not purify unlawful earnings."
"4349","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar concerning the hoarder who joins dirham to dirham and dinar to dinar that his skin will be expanded until every coin is placed separately upon him."
"4350","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no people withhold zakat except that Allah afflicts them with years of drought. > **""No people withhold zakat except that Allah afflicts them with years of famine.""**"
"4351","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports that he went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Madinah, passed a well drawn from, then sheep, then camels, with the Prophet ﷺ warning each owner who neglects its right, then answered what wealth is best and what should suffice them. > **""The owner of this well will carry it on the Day of Resurrection if he does not pay its right. The owner of these sheep will have such and such done to him if he does not pay their right. There is no good in wealth. A servant who serves you, and when he prays he is your brother, or your horse upon which you strive.""**"
"4352","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered sadaqah to be collected and men brought according to their wealth, he wept over the rich taking the reward through charity despite equal prayer and fasting, the Prophet ﷺ answered that the wealthy are lowest except the few who spend freely, then described the fate of camel owners, the three kinds of horse owners, and that concerning mules nothing was revealed except the comprehensive verse of the atom's weight of good and evil. > **""O Abu Dharr, what makes you weep? Rather the wealthy are the lowest, except whoever says with wealth thus and thus, and few are they. There is no owner of camels who does not pay their zakat in their hardship and ease except that they will come on the Day of Resurrection on a flat plain and trample him with their hooves. Whenever the first passes, the last returns to him, until judgment is passed among the people. Horses belong to three groups: whoever keeps them as support in the path of Allah, his hardship and ease are reward for him, and by Allah, even if it breaks its rope and climbs one or two hills or descends upon a green meadow, it is reward; and whoever keeps them for pride, they will be a burden upon him on the Day of Resurrection. Nothing was revealed to me concerning mules except the unique comprehensive verse: Whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.""**"
"4353","Maymun ibn Mihran reports as an athar that when Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما was told Zayd ibn Harithah had died, he asked mercy for him, and when told he left a hundred thousand, he replied that it had not left him."
"4354","'Amr ibn Ya'la ibn Murrah al-Thaqafi reports from his father from his grandfather that a man wearing a large gold ring came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked whether he paid its zakat, and when asked what its zakat is, answered that it is a great ember upon him. > **""Do you pay zakat on this? A great ember upon him.""**"
"4355","Asma' bint Yazid رضي الله عنها reports that she and her maternal aunt entered upon the Prophet ﷺ while her aunt wore gold bracelets, he asked whether they paid its zakat, they said no, and he warned them and commanded payment. > **""Do you pay its zakat? Do you not fear that Allah will bracelet you with bracelets of fire? Pay its zakat.""**"
"4356","'Imran al-Thaqafi reports from his father from his grandfather as an athar that the Prophet ﷺ saw a gold ring and asked whether its zakat is paid, and when asked what its zakat is, answered that it is a great ember. > **""Do you pay its zakat? A great ember.""**"
"4357","Muhammad ibn Ziyad reports as an athar that he heard Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه asked whether sword ornament is among treasures, he said yes, a man called him a witless old man gone in mind, and Abu Umamah replied that he only narrates what he heard."
"4358","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports as an athar that a woman asked him whether zakat is due on her jewelry, he said when it reaches two hundred dirhams pay its zakat, and when she asked whether she may give it to orphans in her care, he said yes."
"4359","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded investing the wealth of orphans so that zakat does not consume it. > **""Trade with the wealth of orphans, lest zakat consume it.""**"
"4360","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar, when asked about the wealth of orphans, that when they mature they should be informed what zakat fell due upon it, and if they wish they pay and if not they do not."
"4361","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the orphan's guardian counts the years, and when the boy reaches puberty tells him that so many years are due upon him."
"4362","Hubayrah ibn Yarim reports as an athar concerning Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه that he used to give them their stipend and then take its zakat."
"4363","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ three qualities in a man that clear him of avarice: willing payment of zakat, hospitality, and giving in calamities. > **""Three qualities, whoever has them is cleared of avarice: whoever pays the zakat of his wealth with a willing soul, entertains the guest, and gives in calamities.""**"
"4364","Khalid ibn Zayd ibn Jariyah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said three qualities protect a man from the stinginess of his soul: paying zakat, entertaining the guest, and giving in hardship. > **""Three qualities, whoever has them is protected from the stinginess of his soul: whoever pays zakat, entertains the guest, and gives in calamity.""**"
"4365","A variant narration from the same companion states that whoever pays zakat, entertains the guest, and gives in calamity is cleared of stinginess. > **""Cleared of stinginess is whoever pays zakat, entertains the guest, and gives in calamity.""**"
"4366","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports as an athar that while a man watered his crop he saw a cloud and heard a voice commanding water for the land of so-and-so, followed the voice until he reached the named land, asked its owner his practice, and he said he returns a third, gives a third in charity, and keeps a third for his family."
"4367","Masruq reports as an athar that Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه used to send word to his land that the same practice be done in it."
"4368","Zirr ibn Hubaysh reports as an athar that Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه had a slave reading the mushaf with companions present, a man called Hasramah asked which ranks of Islam are best, and he answered prayer, then zakat."
"4369","Talhah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is no sadaqah due upon green vegetables. > **""There is no sadaqah upon green vegetables.""**"
"4370","Jabir ibn 'Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that for pasturing horses a dinar is due for every horse. > **""Concerning pasturing horses, for every horse a dinar.""**"
"4371","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ pardon concerning the sadaqah of horses and slaves, and that nothing below two hundred bears zakat. > **""I have pardoned for you the sadaqah of horses and slaves, and there is no zakat in less than two hundred.""**"
"4372","Harithah ibn Mudarrib reports as an athar that people of Syria came to 'Umar رضي الله عنه saying they had acquired horses and slaves and wished zakat and purification therein, 'Umar said his two predecessors had not done so, consulted the companions of Muhammad ﷺ including 'Ali رضي الله عنه, and 'Ali said it is good if it is not a fixed tribute taken after him."
"4373","'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman رضي الله عنه report as an athar that the Prophet ﷺ did not take sadaqah from horses and slaves."
"4374","'Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is no sadaqah upon kas'ah, jabhah and nukhkhah, which Abu 'Umar explains as mules, horses and slaves. > **""There is no sadaqah upon kas'ah, jabhah and nukhkhah.""**"
"4375","Abu Tha'labah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked whether zakat is due on donkeys, and he answered no, except for the unique comprehensive verse of the atom's weight. > **""No, except the unique comprehensive verse: Whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it.""**"
"4376","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command them not to take sadaqah from slaves. > **""Do not take sadaqah from slaves.""**"
"4377","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded concerning a man's or woman's own slaves kept for service without intent to sell that nothing be taken from them, and commanded that sadaqah be taken from those kept for sale. > **""Do not take anything from them as sadaqah. Take sadaqah from that which is kept for sale.""**"
"4378","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fixed the minimums below which no sadaqah is due for camels, silver ounces and grain loads. > **""There is no sadaqah in less than five camels, nor five ounces, nor five wasqs.""**"
"4379","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that no sadaqah is due below five ounces, five camel-heads, or five wasqs. > **""There is no sadaqah in less than five ounces, no sadaqah in less than five camels, and no sadaqah in less than five wasqs.""**"
"4380","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports as an athar on the sunnah from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: the dowry of women is twelve ounces of forty dirhams each, making four hundred eighty; ritual bath is a sa' and ablution two ratls, the sa' being eight ratls; land produce of wheat, barley, raisins and dates bears zakat at five wasqs, the wasq sixty sa's making three hundred sa's by that sa', with nothing below five wasqs."
"4381","Abu Rafi' رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man of Banu Makhzum over sadaqah and stated the minimums for grain, camels and silver. > **""There is no sadaqah in less than five wasqs, nor in less than five camels, nor in less than five ounces.""**"
"4382","Mu'awiyah ibn Haydah al-Qushayri رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said sadaqah is due on every five pasturing camels. > **""In every five pasturing camels is sadaqah.""**"
"4383","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports as an athar that Muhammad ﷺ fixed one dirham in every forty for Muslims' wealth, one in every twenty for dhimmis' wealth, and one in every ten for those with no covenant."
"4384","'Amr ibn Hazm رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to the people of Yemen a letter containing obligations, practices and blood-money, sent with 'Amr ibn Hazm and read to them, addressed from Muhammad the Prophet ﷺ to Shurahbil, al-Harith and Nu'aym ibn 'Abd Kulal, chiefs of Ru'ayn, Mu'afir and Hamdan, granting the fifth of spoils, the tenth on land watered by sky, springs or rain-fed at five wasqs, detailed camel rates from a sheep to two hiqqahs and then per forty and fifty, cattle rates per thirty and forty, sheep rates per forty and hundred, rules of no defective or male animals and no combining or splitting, silver at five dirhams per five ounces and a dirham per forty, gold a dinar per forty, zakat unlawful for Muhammad and his household and for purification of believers, the poor and the path of Allah, none on slaves, farms or collectors when the tenth is paid, and none on a Muslim's slave or horse, with the gravest sins, umrah as lesser hajj, purity for touching the Qur'an, and rulings on divorce, emancipation and prayer dress and hair. > **""In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, from Muhammad the Prophet ﷺ to Shurahbil ibn 'Abd Kulal, al-Harith ibn 'Abd Kulal, Nu'aym ibn 'Abd Kulal, chiefs of Ru'ayn, Mu'afir and Hamdan. Your messenger has returned, you are given Allah's fifth of the spoils, and what Allah prescribed upon the believers of the tenth on landed property, what the sky waters, or is spring-fed, or is rain-fed, therein is the tenth when it reaches five wasqs. In every five pasturing camels a sheep until twenty-four, then a bint makhad, or if absent a male ibn labun, until thirty-five, then a bint labun until forty-five, then a hiqqah fit for breeding until sixty, then a jadha'ah until seventy-five, then two bint labun until ninety, then two hiqqahs fit for breeding until one hundred twenty, then for every forty a bint labun and for every fifty a hiqqah. In every thirty cattle a jadh' or jadha'ah, in every forty cattle a cow. In every forty pasturing sheep a sheep until one hundred twenty, then two sheep until two hundred, then three sheep until three hundred, then for every hundred sheep a sheep. No milk-withheld, aged, emaciated, defective animal nor male goat shall be taken in sadaqah. The scattered shall not be joined nor the joined split to affect sadaqah. Two partners shall settle equally. In every five ounces of silver five dirhams, and in excess a dirham for every forty, with nothing below five ounces. In every forty dinars a dinar. Sadaqah is not lawful for Muhammad nor his household; it is zakat purifying their souls, for the believing poor and in the path of Allah. There is nothing on slaves, farms, or their workers when its sadaqah is paid from the tenth, and there is nothing on a Muslim slave nor his horse. The gravest enormities with Allah on the Day of Resurrection are associating with Allah, killing a believing soul without right, fleeing in Allah's path on the day of battle, disobedience to parents, slandering a chaste woman, learning sorcery, consuming usury, and consuming the orphan's wealth. Umrah is the lesser hajj. None touches the Qur'an except pure. No divorce before possession, no emancipation until purchase. None of you prays in a single garment with his side exposed, nor prays with his hair tied up.""**"
"4385","Malik ibn Aws reports as an athar that he was in the mosque when Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه entered, prayed two rak'ahs by a pillar, was greeted by 'Uthman رضي الله عنه, recited al-Takathur loudly, then told the people he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirm sadaqah on camels, sheep, cattle, wheat, gold, silver and ingots, and that hoarded wealth unspent on Allah's path, debtors and wayfarers becomes a brand on the Day of Resurrection, and when cautioned about people's wealth, cited the verse of those who hoard gold and silver. > **""In camels is their sadaqah, in sheep is their sadaqah, in cattle is their sadaqah, in wheat is its sadaqah, in gold, silver and ingots is their sadaqah. Whoever amasses wealth and does not spend it in the path of Allah, for debtors and the wayfarer, it will be a brand upon him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4386","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ fixed a tenth for what sky and springs water, and half a tenth for what is watered by drawing camels. > **""In what the sky and springs water is the tenth, and in what is watered by drawing camels is half the tenth.""**"
"4387","Qaza'ah reports as an athar that he came to Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه while crowded, waited until people dispersed saying he would not ask what they asked, asked about zakat, and he answered — the narrator not knowing whether he raised it to the Prophet ﷺ — with rates of five dirhams per hundred, sheep from forty to hundreds, and camels from five to one hundred twenty with per fifty and forty thereafter."
"4388","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to his agents on the practice of sadaqat: sheep from forty to one hundred twenty, then two to two hundred, then three to three hundred, then one per hundred; cattle a jadha'ah per thirty and a musinnah per forty; camels a sheep per five to twenty, a bint makhad from twenty-five to thirty-five, a bint labun to forty-five, a hiqqah fit for the stallion to sixty, a jadha'ah to seventy-five, two bint labun to ninety, two hiqqahs to one hundred twenty, then a hiqqah per fifty and a bint labun per forty. > **""In forty sheep up to one hundred twenty. If one more, two sheep up to two hundred. If one more, three sheep up to three hundred. If sheep increase, in every hundred sheep a sheep. In cattle sadaqah: in every thirty cattle a jadha'ah, in every forty cattle a musinnah. In camel sadaqah: in five a sheep, in ten two sheep, in fifteen three sheep, in twenty four sheep, in twenty-five a bint makhad up to thirty-five. If one more, a bint labun up to forty-five. If one more, a hiqqah fit for the stallion up to sixty. If one more, a jadha'ah up to seventy-five. If one more, two bint labun up to ninety. If one more, two hiqqahs up to one hundred twenty. If camels increase, in every fifty a hiqqah and in every forty a bint labun.""**"
"4389","Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him no order concerning the awqas of cattle. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not command me anything concerning the awqas of cattle.""**"
"4390","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Mu'adh رضي الله عنه to Yemen he ordered a tabi' or tabi'ah, jadh' or jadha'ah, for every thirty cattle and a musinnah for every forty, they asked about the awqas, he said he had no order and would ask on return, and the Prophet ﷺ said nothing is due in them. Al-Mas'udi explains the awqas as between thirty and forty, and forty and sixty. > **""There is nothing in them.""**"
"4391","Nafi' reports as an athar that he read the letter of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه: nothing below five camels, one sheep at five to nine, two at ten to fourteen, three at fifteen to nineteen, four at twenty to twenty-four, a bint makhad at twenty-five to thirty-five, a bint labun beyond to forty-five, a hiqqah to sixty, two bint labun to ninety, two hiqqahs to one hundred twenty, then a hiqqah per fifty and a bint labun per forty; nothing on sheep below forty, one at forty to one hundred twenty, two beyond to two hundred, three beyond to three hundred, then one per hundred."
"4392","Al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah رضي الله عنه reports from 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As رضي الله عنه, then young, that they came as a delegation to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he found him foremost in Qur'an including al-Baqarah, appointed him over his companions though youngest, ordered lightness for the weak, old, young, needy when leading, ordered collectors not to take the pregnant, dairy-kept, ram, or a man's food-stock to which he has more right, to touch the Qur'an only pure, and taught that umrah is the lesser hajj, an umrah better than the world, and a hajj better than an umrah. > **""I have appointed you over your companions though you are the youngest. When you lead people, lead them by the weakest among them, for behind you are the old, the young, the weak, and the needy. When you collect sadaqah, do not take the pregnant, nor the dairy-kept, nor the ram of the sheep. A man's slaughter-stock is his by greater right than yours. Do not touch the Qur'an except pure. Know that umrah is the lesser hajj, an umrah is better than the world and what is in it, and a hajj is better than an umrah.""**"
"4393","Salamah ibn al-Akwa' رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that thirty camels are excellent, with one for zakat, one mounted in Allah's path and one gifted, better than forty through a hundred, and woe to the owner of a hundred from the hundred. > **""Excellent camels are thirty: one is given in their zakat, one is mounted in the path of Allah, and one is gifted. They are better than forty, fifty, sixty, eighty, ninety, and a hundred. And woe to the owner of a hundred because of the hundred.""**"
"4394","Ya'la ibn al-Ashdaq reports as an athar that he met several companions of the Prophet ﷺ, among them Ruqad ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه, who said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took from them one sheep per hundred, and two if more."
"4395","Sufyan ibn 'Abdullah reports as an athar that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه sent a collector, he asked whether to count the newborn lambs, the people said he counted but did not take them, and when he returned 'Umar confirmed to count the lamb the shepherd carries but not take it, nor take food-stock, dairy-kept, pregnant, or ram, but take the jadha'ah and thaniyyah, a just middle between low and choice property."
"4396","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is no sadaqah on working cattle, but a tabi' in every thirty and a musinn or musinnah in every forty. > **""There is no sadaqah upon working cattle, but in every thirty a tabi', and in every forty a musinn or musinnah.""**"
"4397","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar that in twenty-five camels a bint makhad is due, and if absent a male ibn labun."
"4398","Al-Dahhak ibn al-Nu'man ibn Sa'd reports that Masruq ibn Wa'il came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-'Aqiq in Madinah, embraced Islam well, asked for a messenger to his people and a letter hoping Allah would guide them, the Prophet ﷺ told Mu'awiyah رضي الله عنه to write it to the chiefs of Hadramawt commanding prayer, zakat, sadaqah on ti'ah and timah, a fifth on buried treasure, a tenth on rain-fed land, forbidding khilat, wirat, shighar, shinaq, janab, jalab, joining two camels in one rope, declaring ijba' as usury and every intoxicant unlawful, and sent Ziyad ibn Labid al-Ansari to them. The terms are then defined: khilat not joining livestock, wirat not valuing by price, shighar exchanging daughters without dowry, shinaq hobbling at resting places, and ijba' selling fruit before safety from blight. > **""In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, to the chiefs of Hadramawt: establish prayer, pay zakat, and sadaqah upon ti'ah and timah. In buried treasure is the fifth, in rain-fed land the tenth. There shall be no khilat, no wirat, no shighar, no shinaq, no janab, no jalab thereby, and two camels shall not be joined in one rope. Whoever practices ijba' has practiced usury. Every intoxicant is unlawful.""**"
"4399","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه and Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent them both to Yemen and commanded them to teach the people the matters of their religion, instructing them concerning sadaqah. > **""Do not take sadaqah except from these four: barley, wheat, raisins, and dates.""**"
"4400","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها, mentioning the affair of Khaybar, reports that the Prophet ﷺ used to send Ibn Rawahah رضي الله عنه to the Jews to estimate the date-palms when the fruit ripened before any of it was eaten, then the Jews were given the choice to take it by that estimate or to hand it over by it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered estimation so that zakat would be reckoned before the fruit was picked and dispersed."
"4401","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ sent Ibn Rawahah رضي الله عنه to Khaybar to estimate for them, then he gave them the choice to take or to return. They said: this is justice; by this the heavens and the earth stand."
"4402","'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm states as an athar that Ibn Rawahah رضي الله عنه estimated for the people of Khaybar for one year only, then he was struck down on the day of Mu'tah. After Ibn Rawahah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would send Jabbar ibn Sakhr ibn Khansa' رضي الله عنه, and he would estimate for them."
"4403","Rafi' ibn Khadij رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Prophet ﷺ used to send Farwah ibn 'Amr رضي الله عنه to estimate the palms. When he entered the garden he would count what was in it of clusters, then calculate one against another for what it contained, and he would not err."
"4404","Jabir رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Prophet ﷺ used to send a man of the Ansar, of Banu Bayadah, called Farwah ibn 'Amr, to estimate the dates of the people of Madinah."
"4405","Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent his father Abu Hathmah رضي الله عنه as an estimator. A man came to the Prophet ﷺ complaining that Abu Hathmah had overestimated against him, so the Prophet ﷺ called Abu Hathmah and asked him about his cousin's claim. Abu Hathmah explained that he had left aside the 'ariyyah of his family, what the poor eat, and what the wind strikes. The Prophet ﷺ addressed the complainant concerning his cousin's fairness. > **""Your cousin has given you extra, and he has been just.""**"
"4406","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports in marfu' form that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the matter she named. > **""He forbade harvesting the date-palms at night.""**"
"4407","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ commanded concerning every walled garden that a cluster be set aside for the mosque. > **""From every garden, a cluster for the mosque.""**"
"4408","Sa'd ibn Abi Dhubab رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him to confirm for his people what they had accepted Islam upon. The Prophet ﷺ did so and appointed him over them, then Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه appointed him after him. He returned to his people and told them there is zakat on honey, for there is no good in wealth that is not purified. They asked him how much he thought, he said the tenth, so he took the tenth from them, brought it to 'Umar رضي الله عنه, informed him of it, and 'Umar took it, sold it, and placed it among the sadaqat of the Muslims."
"4409","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the due on honey and its threshold. > **""In honey is the tenth. In every twelve skins is one skin, and in less than that there is nothing.""**"
"4410","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to Khaybar. A companion of theirs entered a ruin to relieve himself, took a brick to clean himself with, and gold ore collapsed upon him. He took it to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of it. The Prophet ﷺ told him to weigh it, he weighed it and it was two hundred dirhams. The Prophet ﷺ then ruled concerning it. > **""This is rikaz, and in it is the fifth.""**"
"4411","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated what carries no liability and what carries the fifth. Al-Sha'bi explained that rikaz means the ancient buried hoard. > **""The stray beast is void of liability, the well is void of liability, the mine is void of liability, and in rikaz is the fifth.""**"
"4412","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning liability and buried treasure. > **""The mute beast is void of liability, the mine is void of liability, the grazing beast is void of liability, and in rikaz is the fifth.""**"
"4413","Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the due on buried treasure. > **""In rikaz is the fifth.""**"
"4414","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent 'Ali رضي الله عنه as governor over Yemen. He was brought some rikaz, took the fifth from it, gave the remainder to its finder, and when that reached the Prophet ﷺ it pleased him."
"4415","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defined rikaz. > **""Rikaz is gold that grows out of the earth.""**"
"4416","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell a mine appearing in the land of Banu Sulaym called Fir'awn and Fir'an — in the speech of Abu Jahm it is near al-Sawa' — to which the worst of people will go out, or to which the worst of people will be gathered, the narrator doubting the wording. > **""A mine will appear in the land of Banu Sulaym called Fir'awn and Fir'an. The worst of people will go out to it — or the worst of people will be gathered to it.""**"
"4417","Sarra' bint Nabhan al-Ghanawiyyah رضي الله عنها reports that a clan dug in the dwelling-place of Kilab and found an ancient hoard. Kilab said it was their dwelling, the clan said they had dug it, so they brought the dispute to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He judged it for the clan and took the fifth from them. She states they bought with their share one hundred head of livestock, brought them to the clan, and the collector wanted to take sadaqah from them. They refused him and came to the Prophet ﷺ, who told them about using it otherwise and about nothing being due that year, and about the collector's pleasure and anger. > **""If you have put them into other than them, then there is nothing upon you in this year. Indeed when the collector leaves the people while pleased with them, Allah is pleased with them, and when he leaves while angry with them, Allah is angry with them.""**"
"4418","Al-Hasan states as a balagh that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning liability and buried treasure. > **""The mine is void of liability, the mute beast is void of liability, the well is void of liability, and in rikaz is the fifth.""**"
"4419","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was brought a piece of gold which was the first sadaqah to come to him from a mine of theirs, and he foretold what would come with mines. > **""There will surely be mines, and in them will be the worst of creation.""**"
"4420","Umm Sa'd al-Ansariyyah رضي الله عنها, the wife of Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the condition for zakat on newly acquired wealth. > **""There is no zakat upon one who acquires wealth until a year has passed over it.""**"
"4421","Talhah ibn 'Ubaydillah رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took in advance the sadaqah of al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه for two years."
"4422","'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Prophet ﷺ took in advance from al-'Abbas رضي الله عنه the sadaqah of two years, with the added wording that a man's paternal uncle stands in his father's place. > **""A man's paternal uncle is like his father.""**"
"4423","Abu Rafi' رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent 'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه as a collector over sadaqah. He came to al-'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه, who spoke harshly to him, so 'Umar came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him. The Prophet ﷺ reminded him of the standing of the paternal uncle and informed him that al-'Abbas had already advanced the charity. > **""O 'Umar, did you not know that a man's paternal uncle stands in his father's place? Indeed al-'Abbas advanced to us the sadaqah of the year a year before.""**"
"4424","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded where the sadaqah of the desert people is to be taken. > **""The sadaqah of the people of the desert is taken at their waters and in their courtyards.""**"
"4425","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning the coming of the collector. > **""When the collector comes, he should not leave except while pleased with you.""**"
"4426","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold the coming of disliked riders and commanded welcoming them, leaving them to what they seek, pleasing them, and seeking their supplication, explaining justice and injustice and the completion of zakat. > **""There will come to you disliked riders. When they come to you, welcome them, leave them to what they seek. If they are just, it is for themselves, and if they wrong, it is upon them. Please them, for the completion of your zakat is their pleasure, and let them supplicate for you.""**"
"4427","'Abdullah ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that a man of the Ansar came to the Prophet ﷺ and said they had been commanded zakat, the zakat of fitr, which they pay, asking what should become of them if they meet rulers who do not place it in its places. The Prophet ﷺ commanded payment and accountability. > **""Pay it to your rulers, for they will be held to account for it.""**"
"4428","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command payment to the rulers with a condition. > **""Pay it to them as long as they pray the five prayers.""**"
"4429","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه states as an athar concerning zakat al-fitr that upon every free person and slave, male and female, young and old, poor and rich, is a sa' of dates or half a sa' of wheat. Ma'mar said it reached him that al-Zuhri used to attribute it to the Prophet ﷺ."
"4430","'Amr ibn 'Awf رضي الله عنه states as an athar from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to command zakat al-fitr before the Eid prayer is performed, reciting the verse: He has surely succeeded who purifies himself, remembers the name of his Lord, and prays."
"4431","Khusaylah bint Wa'ilah رضي الله عنها states as an athar that she heard her father recite the verse: He has surely succeeded who purifies himself, remembers the name of his Lord, and prays. He explained it as giving wheat on the day of fitr before the prayer at the place of prayer."
"4432","'Amr ibn 'Awf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the due of zakat upon the Muslims. > **""Zakat upon the Muslims is a sa' of dates, or a sa' of raisins, or a sa' of barley, or a sa' of aqit.""**"
"4433","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ ordered a crier to call out in the valley of Makkah commanding sadaqat al-fitr, saying it is a due obligation upon every Muslim, with its measure, warning that the child belongs to the bed and the adulterer gets the stone. In another narration the measure of wheat and acceptance of flour and sawiq were added. > **""It is a due obligation upon every Muslim, male or female, young or old, free or slave, resident or Bedouin: two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of other food. Verily the child belongs to the bed, and for the adulterer is the stone. Or half a sa' of wheat. Whoever brings flour, it is accepted from him, and whoever brings sawiq, it is accepted from him.""**"
"4434","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him commanding him to pay his head zakat with the people even in scarcity. In the wording of al-Kabir and al-Awsat it is even if only a thread is found. > **""O Zayd, pay the zakat of your head with the people, even if you find only a sa' of wheat.""**"
"4435","Aws ibn al-Hadathan رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded bringing sadaqat al-fitr as a sa' of food. The narrator added that their food in those days was wheat, dates, and raisins, with aqit in another narration. > **""Bring sadaqat al-fitr, a sa' of food.""**"
"4436","Jabir ibn 'Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the measure of sadaqat al-fitr for every person in flour, wheat, barley, and sweet foods. > **""Sadaqat al-fitr upon every person is two mudds of flour or wheat, a sa' of barley, and of sweet foods a sa' of raisins or a sa' of dates.""**"
"4437","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took zakat al-fitr from the people of the desert as aqit."
"4438","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه states as an athar that he saw some Arabs come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying they were owners of livestock whose sadaqah they pay, asking whether it would suffice them for the zakat of Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered them that it would not, commanding payment for each person as purification. > **""No. Pay it for the young and the old, the free and the slave, for it is a purification for you.""**"
"4439","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما states as an athar that they used to eat and drink, pay sadaqat al-fitr, then go out to the place of prayer."
"4440","Asma' bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنهما states as an athar that in the time of the Messenger ﷺ she used to pay on behalf of her household, free and owned, two mudds of wheat or a sa' of dates by the mudd by which they traded for food."
"4441","Asma' bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنهما states as an athar in another narration that they used to pay zakat al-fitr in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ by the mudd by which the people of Madinah traded for food, and all the people of Madinah did that."
"4442","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه states as an athar concerning zakat al-fitr that it is two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates or barley."
"4443","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in her house when a man came asking the amount of sadaqah for such-and-such. The Prophet ﷺ told him, the man complained that so-and-so had transgressed against him, and investigation found he had transgressed by a sa'. The Prophet ﷺ warned them about a greater transgressor to come. The people were alarmed and discussed it until one of them asked what a man absent among his camels, livestock, and crops should do if he pays his zakat yet is transgressed against while absent from the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ answered concerning sincere payment, prayer, and fighting with his weapon. > **""How will you be when there pursues you one who transgresses against you more severely than this transgression? Whoever pays the zakat of his wealth willingly, seeking thereby the Face of Allah and the Abode of the Hereafter, concealing nothing of his wealth, establishing prayer, then paying zakat, yet is transgressed against concerning the right, then takes his weapon and fights and is killed, he is a martyr.""**"
"4444","Jarir ibn Hazim reports that an old man sat with them in the shop of Ayyub while people talked, saying his master had narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Asked for his name, he said Qurrah ibn Du'mus al-Numayri رضي الله عنه. Qurrah said he came to Madinah, approached the Prophet ﷺ among the people, could not draw near, so he called out asking him to seek forgiveness for the Numayri lad, and the Prophet ﷺ forgave him. He then reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent al-Dahhak ibn Qays رضي الله عنه as a collector, who returned with excellent camels. The Prophet ﷺ questioned him about taking the best wealth of Hilal ibn 'Amir, Numayr ibn 'Amir, and 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah. He explained he had heard mention of battle and wanted to bring mounts to ride and carry upon. The Prophet ﷺ swore what was left was dearer to him than what was taken, ordered return, and commanded taking from the edges. Jarir said the Muslims called those camels the aged striving ones. > **""May Allah forgive you. Did you go to Hilal ibn 'Amir, Numayr ibn 'Amir, and 'Amir ibn Rabi'ah and take the best of their wealth? By Allah, what you left is dearer to me than what you took. Return them, and take from the edges of their wealth and their sadaqat.""**"
"4445","Salim ibn Abi Umayyah Abu al-Nadr reports that an old man of Banu Tamim sat with him in the mosque of Basrah in the time of al-Hajjaj, holding a document, asking whether it would avail him with the ruler. Asked what it was, he said it was a writing from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that none should transgress against them in their sadaqat. Salim doubted it would avail, and asked its story. The old man said he had come to Madinah as a young lad with his father with camels to sell, his father being a friend of Talhah ibn 'Ubaydillah al-Tamimi رضي الله عنه. His father asked Talhah to sell the camels for him. Talhah explained that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden a resident to sell for a Bedouin, but he would sit nearby while they offered their camels, advising them when satisfied with a buyer's fidelity and truthfulness. They went to the market, men bargained until a satisfactory offer came, his father asked Talhah whether to sell, Talhah approved, they sold, took their money, and finished their need. His father then asked Talhah to obtain for them a writing from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that none should transgress against them in their sadaqat. Talhah said that protection was for them and every Muslim, but the father still wished to have a writing. Talhah brought them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, introduced the Bedouin friend seeking a writing against transgression in his sadaqah, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed it was for him and every Muslim, Talhah repeated his wish for a writing, so the Prophet ﷺ wrote this document for them. > **""This is for him and for every Muslim.""**"
"4446","Jarir رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning one who transgresses in sadaqah. > **""The one who transgresses in sadaqah is like the one who withholds it.""**"
"4447","'Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked faith with trust and likened the transgressor to the withholder. > **""There is no faith for one who has no trust, and the one who transgresses in sadaqah is like the one who withholds it.""**"
"4448","Al-Sunabihi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a fine she-camel among the sadaqah camels and supplicated against its collector. The man explained he had exchanged it for two camels from the edges of the herd, and the Prophet ﷺ approved. > **""May Allah destroy the owner of this she-camel. Then good.""**"
"4449","Rafi' ibn Khadij رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ liken the just collector working for Allah to the fighter until his return to his family. > **""The worker over sadaqah with truth, for the sake of Allah the Exalted, is like the fighter in the path of Allah the Exalted until he returns to his family.""**"
"4450","'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the employed worker who takes the right and gives the right as remaining like the mujahid until he returns to his house. > **""When the worker is employed, takes the right and gives the right, he remains like the mujahid in the path of Allah until he returns to his house.""**"
"4451","'Adi ibn Hatim رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Prophet ﷺ sent him as a collector to his people, and when he had taken their sadaqat, the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ coincided with that."
"4452","'Uqbah ibn 'Amir رضي الله عنه states as an athar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him as a collector, he sought permission to eat from the sadaqah, and permission was given to them."
"4453","Salamah al-Hamdani رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to Qays ibn Malik al-Arhabi رضي الله عنه appointing him over his people, assigning him produce and raisins as a continuing grant for him and his progeny. Qays said the Prophet's words ""forever, forever"" were dearest to him, hoping his progeny would ever remain. Yahya explained that their 'urb means the desert people and their khumur means the villagers. > **""In Your Name, O Allah. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Qays ibn Malik. Peace upon you, and the mercy of Allah, His blessings, and His forgiveness. As for what follows: I appoint you over your people, their desert folk and their villagers, their clients and their attendants. I grant you from the produce of Yasar two hundred sa's, and from the raisins of Khaywan two hundred sa's, running for you and your progeny after you forever, forever.""**"
"4454","'Ali رضي الله عنه reports that the sadaqah camels passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he took a hair from a camel's back, and declared his share in it. > **""I have no more right to this hair than any man among the Muslims.""**"
"4455","Mas'ud ibn Qabisah — or Qabisah ibn Mas'ud, the narrator doubting — reports that this clan of Muharib prayed the dawn prayer, and when they finished, a youth among them said he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell the opening of the easts and wests of the earth and the fate of their officials except the God-fearing and trustworthy. > **""The easts of the earth and its wests will surely be opened for you, and their officials will be in the Fire except whoever fears Allah the Exalted and fulfills the trust.""**"
"4456","Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed him over the sadaqah of Banu so-and-so, warning him against coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying a young camel on his shoulder — or his upper back, the narrator doubting — bellowing. He asked to be excused, and the Prophet ﷺ excused him. > **""Take charge of the sadaqah of Banu so-and-so, and beware lest you come on the Day of Resurrection carrying a young camel on your shoulder bellowing on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4457","Hulb رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned sadaqah and warned against coming with misappropriated livestock. > **""None of you should ever come with a sheep that bleats.""**"
"4458","'Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described holding them back from the Fire while they rush into it like moths and locusts, his preceding them at the Hawd, recognizing them by their marks and names as a man recognizes a strange camel among his camels, their being taken to the left, his intercession, Allah's reply that they turned back after him, and his disavowal of whoever comes carrying misappropriated animals and goods calling for help after conveyance. > **""I hold your waists back from the Fire: come away from the Fire, come away from the Fire. Yet you overpower me, rushing into it like the rushing of moths or locusts. Soon I shall release your waists. I am your forerunner at the Hawd. You will come to me together and in groups. I will know you by your marks and your names, as a man knows a strange camel among his camels. You will be taken to the left. I will plead for you with the Lord of the worlds, saying: My Lord, my people; my Lord, my ummah. He will say: O Muhammad, you do not know what they innovated after you; they kept walking backward upon their heels after you. Let me never know one of you on the Day of Resurrection carrying a sheep that bleats, calling: O Muhammad, O Muhammad, and I say: I avail you nothing; I conveyed to you. Let me never know one of you on the Day of Resurrection carrying a camel that bellows, calling: O Muhammad, O Muhammad, and I say: I avail you nothing; I conveyed to you. Let me never know one of you coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying a horse that neighs, calling: O Muhammad, O Muhammad, and I say: I avail you nothing; I conveyed to you. Let me never know one of you on the Day of Resurrection carrying a leather waterskin, calling: O Muhammad, O Muhammad, and I say: I avail you nothing; I conveyed to you.""**"
"4459","'A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man as a collector called Ibn al-Lutbiyyah. He collected, returned to the Prophet ﷺ claiming he had left no right for them and had accepted a gift given to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat upon the minbar, described such collectors, asked why they did not sit in their mothers' houses to see who gifts them, warned against coming with bellowing, mooing, and bleating animals, then raised his hands until the whiteness of his armpits was seen. > **""I send men over sadaqah, then one of them comes saying: By Allah, I did not transgress nor leave any right for you, but I was gifted and accepted the gift. Why did he not sit in his mother's house and see who gifts him? Beware lest one of you come on his shoulder with a camel that bellows, a cow that moos, or a sheep that bleats.""**"
"4460","Ibn 'Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man as a collector called Ibn al-Lutbiyyah. He collected, returned claiming he had not transgressed nor left their right, but had accepted a gift. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat upon the minbar, described such collectors, asked why they did not sit in their mothers' houses to see who gifts them, warned against coming with bellowing, mooing, and bleating animals, raised his hands until the whiteness of his armpits was seen, then declared conveyance. > **""I send men over sadaqah, then one of them comes saying: By Allah, I did not transgress nor leave their right, but I was gifted and accepted the gift. Why did he not sit in his mother's house and see who gifts him? Beware lest one of you come on his neck with a camel that bellows, a cow that moos, or a sheep that bleats. O Allah, have I conveyed?""**"
"4461","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Sa'd ibn 'Ubadah رضي الله عنه as a collector, warning him against coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying a bellowing camel. He said he could not bear it and asked to be excused, so the Prophet ﷺ excused him. > **""O Sa'd, beware lest you come on the Day of Resurrection carrying a camel that bellows.""**"
"4462","'Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him over sadaqah, warning him against coming on the Day of Resurrection carrying bellowing, mooing, and bleating animals. He asked whether that would truly be so, the Prophet ﷺ swore by Him in Whose Hand is his soul, so he swore by Him Who sent the Prophet ﷺ with the truth that he would never work for him in anything. > **""O Abu al-Walid, fear Allah. Do not come on the Day of Resurrection carrying a camel that bellows, a cow that moos, or a sheep that bleats. Yes, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul.""**"
"4463","Abu Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him as a collector, he warned him against coming on the Day of Resurrection with a misappropriated sadaqah camel upon his back. Abu Mas'ud said he would not proceed on that mission, and the Prophet ﷺ said he would not compel him. > **""Look, O Abu Mas'ud, may I never find you coming on the Day of Resurrection with a bellowing camel of the sadaqah camels upon your back which you misappropriated. Then I shall not compel you.""**"
"4464","Jahm ibn Fadalah states as an athar that he entered the mosque of Damascus and found Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه removing lice and burying them. He sat with him, Abu Umamah glorified Allah thrice, praised Him thrice, and magnified Him thrice, then said they are light upon the tongue, heavy in the Scale, ascending to the Most Merciful. Jahm said he was a desert man and the collectors transgress against them. Abu Umamah told him sadaqah is a right and its seekers are in the Fire by the word of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether they fall short or transgress, that they should bring the wealth hiding nothing lest they spoil what they hide and what they bring, that they should not revile the collectors when they see them but seek refuge in Allah from their evil."
"4465","Jahm states as an athar in another narration that he asked Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه mentioning the workers over sadaqah, and he said sadaqah is a right and its workers are in the Fire by the word of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"4466","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that when he sent collectors over sadaqat, he commanded them to place what they took first among the near kin of those from whom it was taken, then their clan if no kin, then the needy among neighbors and others. > **""What is taken of the sadaqat shall be placed first among the near kin of those from whom it was taken, then their clan, then the needy among neighbors and others.""**"
"4467","Mutarrif ibn 'Abdillah ibn al-Shikhkhir reports from a man of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ who was a governor in Kufah that he addressed the people one day saying that giving this wealth is a trial and withholding it is a trial, and that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained engaged with it until he finished, then came down."
"4468","Abu al-Fayd states as an athar that he witnessed Mu'awiyah رضي الله عنه give al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad a donkey, whereupon a man of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ called al-'Irbad ibn Sariyah رضي الله عنه stood and said he had no right to take it nor Mu'awiyah to give it, as if looking at him on the Day of Resurrection carrying it upon his neck with its head downward."
"4469","Sa'id ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command the Arabs to praise Allah for lifting the ushur from them. > **""O company of Arabs, praise Allah Who has lifted the ushur from you.""**"
"4470","Malik ibn 'Atahiyah رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ command killing the tithe collector, meaning thereby sadaqah taken without right. In the wording of al-Kabir it is sadaqah he takes without right. > **""When you see a tithe collector, kill him.""**"
"4471","Al-Hasan states as an athar that 'Uthman ibn Abi al-'As رضي الله عنه passed by Kilab ibn Umayyah sitting at the seat of the tithe collector in Basrah, asking what seated him there. He said Ziyad had employed him there. 'Uthman offered to narrate a hadith he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he accepted, so 'Uthman narrated that Allah's Prophet Dawud عليه السلام had an hour of the night in which he would wake his household saying to pray, for it is an hour in which Allah answers supplication except for a sorcerer or a tithe collector. Kilab ibn Umayyah then boarded a ship, went to Ziyad, sought release, and was released. > **""Dawud, the Prophet of Allah, had an hour of the night in which he would wake his household, saying: O household of Dawud, rise and pray, for this is an hour in which Allah answers supplication except for a sorcerer or a tithe collector.""**"
"4472","The Prophet ﷺ states in the wording of al-Awsat that the gates of heaven are opened at midnight, a caller calls asking for supplicants, askers, and the distressed, and no Muslim supplicates but Allah answers him except an adulteress who traffics with her private part, or a tithe collector. > **""The gates of heaven are opened at midnight. A caller calls: Is there a supplicant whose prayer shall be answered? Is there an asker who shall be given? Is there a distressed one who shall be relieved? No Muslim prays a prayer but Allah the Exalted answers him, except an adulteress who traffics with her private part, or a tithe collector.""**"
"4473","The Prophet ﷺ states in the wording of al-Kabir that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say Allah draws near to His creation and forgives whoever seeks forgiveness except an adulteress with her private part, or a tithe collector. > **""Indeed Allah draws near to His creation and forgives whoever seeks forgiveness, except an adulteress with her private part, or a tithe collector.""**"
"4474","Abu al-Khayr reported that Maslamah ibn Mukhallad, who was governor over Egypt, offered Ruwayfi' ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه that he would appoint him over the ushur, so he replied that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, the collector of the maks will be in the Fire.""** By it is meant the 'ashir, the one who takes unjust tithes."
"4475","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما, when he saw Suhayl, would say, may Allah curse Suhayl, for he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""He was a tithe-collector among the tithe-collectors of Yemen who wronged them, so Allah transformed him and placed him where you see.""**"
"4476","The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned Suhayl and said: > **""He was an oppressive tithe-collector, so Allah transformed him into a shooting star and placed him where you see.""**"
"4477","I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""He was a tithe-collector who wronged them and seized their wealth, so Allah transformed him into a shooting star and placed him where you see.""**"
"4478","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ cursed Suhayl three times, for he used to take ushur from the people, so Allah transformed him into a shooting star. > **""May Allah curse Suhayl.""**"
"4479","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that a funeral passed by the Prophet ﷺ, so he said: > **""Glad tidings for him, if he was not an 'arif.""** By 'arif is meant a state official set over people."
"4480","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, in the Fire there is a rock called Wayl; the 'urafa will climb upon it and descend into it.""**"
"4481","Mawdud ibn al-Harith, from his father, from his grandfather, that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, a man of Banu Tamim took all my wealth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, I have nothing with me to give you. Then he said, would you like to be made 'arif over your people. He replied, no. He said: > **""Indeed, the 'arif will be thrust into the Fire with force.""**"
"4482","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ found a date beneath his side at night and ate it, then did not sleep that night. One of his wives said, O Messenger of Allah, were you sleepless last night. He said: > **""I found a date beneath my side and ate it, and we had dates of charity with us, so I feared it might have been from it.""**"
"4483","Abu Umayr or Abu Umayrah رضي الله عنه reported that they were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a man came with a tray of dates. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked what it was, charity or gift. He said, charity. So he passed it to the people, while Hasan رضي الله عنه was crawling before him, and the child took a date and put it in his mouth. The Prophet ﷺ put his finger in the child's mouth, removed the date and cast it aside, then said: > **""Indeed, we, the Household of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for us.""**"
"4484","Ata ibn al-Sa'ib reported that Umm Kulthum, daughter of Ali رضي الله عنها, told him when he brought her something of charity that had been ordered for her, that they warn their youths against it, for Maymun or Mihran, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, told her that he passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said to him: > **""O Maymun, indeed, we are a Household forbidden from charity, and indeed our freedmen are from ourselves, so do not eat charity.""**"
"4485","Abu al-Hawra reported that they were with al-Hasan ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما and he was asked what he retained from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, I was walking with him when he passed a threshing-floor of charity dates, so I took a date and put it in my mouth, and he took it with its saliva. One of the people said, what harm if you had left it. He said: > **""Indeed, we, the Household of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for us.""** He also said, I retained from him the five prayers."
"4486","Rabi'ah ibn Shayban Abu al-Hawra reported that he said to al-Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما, what do you retain from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said, I climbed a chamber, took a date and chewed it in my mouth, so the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Throw it away, for charity is not lawful for us.""**"
"4487","Salman رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to accept the gift and did not accept charity. > **""The Prophet ﷺ accepted the gift and did not accept charity.""**"
"4488","Salman al-Farisi رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ with food while he was a slave, and said, this is charity. So he ordered his Companions and they ate, but he did not eat. Then he came with food and said, this is a gift I honour you with, for I saw you do not eat charity. So he ordered his Companions, they ate, and he ate with them. > **""The Prophet ﷺ did not eat charity, but he ate the gift with his Companions.""**"
"4489","Salman رضي الله عنه came to the Prophet ﷺ with a tray bearing fresh dates. He asked what it was. He said, charity for you and your Companions. He said, O Salman, we do not eat charity. So Salman took it away. The next day he came with a tray of fresh dates. He asked what this tray was. He said, a gift. So he said to his Companions: > **""O Salman, indeed, we do not eat charity.""** > **""Come near and eat.""**"
"4490","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that when food was brought to the Prophet ﷺ from other than his family, he asked about it. If it was said to be a gift, he ate. If it was said to be charity, he said: > **""Eat.""** And he himself did not eat."
"4491","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ sent Arqam ibn Abi al-Arqam al-Zuhri رضي الله عنه over some charity, and he passed by Abu Rafi' رضي الله عنه and took him along. Then he came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him, so he said: > **""O Abu Rafi', indeed, charity is forbidden for Muhammad and the Household of Muhammad, and indeed the freedman of a people is from them.""**"
"4492","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that some youths of Banu Hashim came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, employ us over charity, we will take therefrom what people take and pay as they pay. He said: > **""Indeed, we, the Household of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for us, and it is the filth of people. But what do you think, when I take hold of the ring of the gate of Paradise, will I prefer anyone over you.""**"
"4493","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Charity is not lawful for us nor for our freedmen.""**"
"4494","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Nawfal ibn al-Harith sent his two sons to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said to them, go to your cousin, perhaps he will employ you over charities so you may gain something and marry. They met Ali رضي الله عنه, who asked where they were going, so they told him their need. He told them to return, and in the evening they were ordered to go to the Prophet ﷺ. When they reached the door and sought permission, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to A'ishah رضي الله عنها, lower your curtain, my two cousins will enter upon me. They told the Prophet ﷺ their need, so the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Nothing of the charities is lawful for you, O Household, nor the washings of people's hands. Indeed, for you in the fifth of the fifth is what will enrich you or suffice you.""**"
"4495","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that a woman gifted her a sheep's leg, though it had been given to that woman as charity, so the Prophet ﷺ ordered her to accept it. > **""Accept it.""**"
"4496","Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Bakr رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Charity is not lawful for a rich man, nor for one of sound strength.""**"
"4497","Ubaydullah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar reported that two men came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the Farewell Pilgrimage asking him of charity. He raised his gaze to them and lowered it, and saw them to be two sturdy men, so he said: > **""If you wish, I will help you, but there is no share therein for a rich man nor for a strong earner.""**"
"4498","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Charity is not lawful for a rich man, nor for one of sound strength.""**"
"4499","A man of Banu Hilal reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Begging is not lawful for a rich man, nor for one of sound strength.""**"
"4500","Mina reported that they came to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه in the time of Uthman رضي الله عنه and said, give us our stipends. He said, you have no stipend with me; your stipend is from your fay and your jizyah, and charity belongs to its people. When they kept returning to him, he brought the keys to Uthman and cast them down, and said, I will not be a treasurer."
"4501","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The poor is not the wanderer, nor the one who is turned away with one date or two, nor one morsel or two, but the poor is the abstinent who does not ask people and is not noticed so that charity is given to him.""**"
"4502","Ibn Abi Mulaykah reported that the bridle sometimes fell from the hand of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه, so he would strike his she-camel's foreleg to make her kneel and take it himself. They said to him, why did you not order us so we could hand it to you. He said, my Beloved ﷺ commanded me not to ask people for anything."
"4503","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took my pledge upon five, took firm covenant upon seven, and made Allah witness upon nine, that I would not fear the blame of any blamer in Allah. Abu al-Muthanna reported that Abu Dharr said, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called me and said, will you pledge, and for you is Paradise. I said, yes, and stretched my hand, while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stipulated upon me that I would not ask people for anything. I said, yes. He said: > **""Will you pledge, and for you is Paradise.""** > **""Not even your whip, if it falls from you, until you dismount and take it yourself.""**"
"4504","In a narration the Prophet ﷺ said, six days, then comprehend, O Abu Dharr, what will be said to you after. When the seventh day came, he said: > **""I enjoin upon you fear of Allah in your secret affair and your open, and when you do evil then do good, and do not ask anyone for anything even if your whip falls, and do not take a trust, and do not judge between two.""**"
"4505","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, who will pledge. So Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said, upon what shall we pledge, have we not already pledged once, O Messenger of Allah. He said, upon that you ask no one for anything. Thawban said, what is for him thereby, O Messenger of Allah. He said, Paradise. So Thawban pledged to him. Abu Umamah said, I saw him at Makkah amid a crowd of people when his whip fell while he was riding, sometimes falling upon a man's shoulder, and the man would take it to hand it to him, but he would not take it from him until he himself dismounted and took it. > **""Upon that you ask no one for anything.""** > **""Paradise.""**"
"4506","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported in a narration that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat one day among a group of his Companions, raised his hand and said three times, who will pledge to me, and none stood to him except Thawban, then he mentioned the like thereof. > **""Who will pledge to me.""**"
"4507","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that his intimate friend ﷺ counselled him with seven: love of the poor and nearness to them, looking to one below me and not to one above me, joining kin though they cut off, increasing in la hawla wa la quwwata illa billah, speaking the bitter truth, not fearing the blame of any blamer in Allah, and not asking people for anything. > **""Do not ask people for anything.""**"
"4508","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If the owner of begging knew what is therein for him, he would not beg.""**"
"4509","Umm Sinan al-Aslamiyyah رضي الله عنها, who was among the pledging women, reported that she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, I have come to you in shyness, and I did not come until driven by need. He said: > **""If you had sufficed yourself, it would have been better for you.""**"
"4510","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Dispense with people, even with a fragment of the siwak.""**"
"4511","A'ishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""That one of you take a rope and eat thereby and give charity is better than that he ask people, whether they give him or withhold.""**"
"4512","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that two men came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him, so he said, go to these ravines, gather firewood and sell it. So they went and gathered firewood, came and sold it, and gained food. Then they went and gathered again, and kept on until they bought two garments, then bought two donkeys, and said, Allah has blessed for us in the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Go to these ravines, gather firewood and sell it.""**"
"4513","Abu Salamah ibn Abdur Rahman, from his father رضي الله عنه, reported that he had a promise with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when Qurayzah was conquered he came so that he would fulfil what he had promised him, but he heard him say: > **""Whoever seeks independence, Allah will make him independent, and whoever is content, Allah will make him content.""** So he said to himself, surely I will not ask him ﷺ for anything."
"4514","Ali رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, whoever begs after sufficiency increases thereby from the embers of Hell. They said, what is sufficiency. He said: > **""Whoever begs after sufficiency increases thereby from the embers of Hell.""** > **""A night's supper.""**"
"4515","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that Umar رضي الله عنه said, O Messenger of Allah, I heard so-and-so praising and mentioning that you gave them two dinars. The Prophet ﷺ said, by Allah, but so-and-so is not so; I gave him between ten and a hundred, yet he does not say that. Indeed, one of you leaves me with his begging carrying it under his arm, meaning fire. Umar said, O Messenger of Allah, why do you give them. He said: > **""By Allah, I gave him between ten and a hundred, yet he does not say that. Indeed, one of you leaves me with his begging carrying it under his arm, meaning fire.""** > **""What shall I do. They refuse except that, and Allah refuses for me miserliness.""**"
"4516","Umar رضي الله عنه reported that two men entered upon the Prophet ﷺ asking him for something, so he helped them with two dinars, and they left praising good. I entered upon him and said, O Messenger of Allah, I saw so-and-so leaving you praising good. He said, but so-and-so does not say that, though I gave him between ten and a hundred, yet he does not say that. And one of you leaves me with his charity carrying it under his arm, and it is fire for him. I said, O Messenger of Allah, how do you give him while knowing it is fire for him. He said: > **""One of you leaves me with his charity carrying it under his arm, and it is fire for him.""** > **""What shall I do. They come to me asking me, and Allah Almighty refuses for me miserliness.""**"
"4517","Ja'far ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hakam, from his father, from a man of Muzaynah, that his mother said to him, shall we not go and ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as people ask him. So I went to ask him and found him standing preaching, saying: > **""Whoever abstains, Allah will keep him chaste, and whoever seeks independence, Allah will enrich him, and whoever asks people while having the equivalent of five uqiyas has begged importunately.""** He said to himself, a she-camel of ours is better than five uqiyas, and another she-camel of so-and-so is better, so he returned and did not ask him."
"4518","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No one of you opens upon himself a door of begging except Allah opens upon him a door of poverty.""**"
"4519","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما raised the report to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: > **""Do not persist in begging, for whoever extracts thereby something from us, no blessing will be in it for him.""**"
"4520","Sahl ibn al-Hanzaliyyah al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that Uyaynah and al-Aqra' asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for something, so he ordered Mu'awiyah رضي الله عنه to write it for them, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sealed it and ordered him to hand it to them. As for Uyaynah, he asked what was in it, was told it was what had been ordered, so he accepted it and tied it in his turban, and he was the more forbearing of the two. As for al-Aqra', he said, shall I carry a document I know not what is in it, like the document of al-Mutalammis. Mu'awiyah told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ their sayings. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out for a need and passed by a camel kneeling at the mosque door in the morning, then passed by it at the end of the day and said, where is the owner of this camel. He was sought but not found, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, fear Allah regarding these beasts, ride them healthy and ride them fat. Then in displeasure he said, whoever asks while having what suffices him only increases from the embers of Hell. They said, O Messenger of Allah, what suffices him. He said: > **""Where is the owner of this camel.""** > **""Fear Allah regarding these beasts, ride them healthy and ride them fat. Indeed, whoever asks while having what suffices him only increases from the embers of Hell.""** > **""What gives him his morning meal or his evening meal.""**"
"4521","Mutarrif ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir reported from a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ who was governor at Kufah, that he preached saying, in giving this wealth there is trial, and in withholding it there is trial, and therefore the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood with it until he finished, then descended."
"4522","Thawban رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever begs while independent of it, it will be something on his face on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4523","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The begging of the rich is a disgrace on his face on the Day of Resurrection.""** > **""The begging of the rich is fire; if given little, then little, and if given much, then much.""**"
"4524","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Begging is scratches on the face of its owner on the Day of Resurrection, so whoever wishes may preserve his face. And the easiest of begging is the begging of kin one asks for a need, and the best of begging is begging after sufficiency, and begin with those you support.""**"
"4525","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever begs while independent of begging will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection with scratches on his face.""**"
"4526","Hubshi ibn Junadah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever begs without poverty, it is as if he eats ember.""**"
"4527","I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever asks people without a calamity befalling him, it is as if he chews ember.""**"
"4528","Mas'ud ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The servant keeps asking while rich until his face is worn away, so he will have no face with Allah.""**"
"4529","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Begging is not right for a rich man except from kin or a ruler.""** > **""Begging is a scratch with which a man scratches his face, except one asks a ruler or in a matter unavoidable.""**"
"4530","Umm al-Darda رضي الله عنها reported from Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه that she said to him, why do you not seek as so-and-so seeks. He said, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Beyond you is a steep pass which the burdened will not cross, so I love to lighten myself for that pass.""**"
"4531","Hakim ibn Hizam رضي الله عنه reported that wealth came from Bahrain, so the Prophet ﷺ called al-Abbas رضي الله عنه and scooped for him, then said, shall I increase you. He said, yes, so he scooped for him, then said, shall I increase you. He said, yes, so he scooped for him, then said, shall I increase you. He said, yes. He said, leave for those after you. Then he called me and scooped for me, so I said, O Messenger of Allah, is it good for me or evil for me. He said, rather evil for you. So I returned to him what he had given me, then said, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, I will not accept a gift from anyone after you. Muhammad, meaning Ibn Sirin, reported that Hakim said, I said, O Messenger of Allah, supplicate Allah to bless me. He said: > **""Shall I increase you.""** > **""Leave for those after you.""** > **""No, rather it is evil for you.""** > **""O Allah, bless for him in his hand's bargain.""**"
"4532","In another narration with him, he assisted with two horses on the day of Hunayn, and they were lost, so he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, O Messenger of Allah, my two horses were lost, so compensate me. So he gave him, and he asked for more. > **""Compensate me.""**"
"4533","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, hands are three: the Hand of Allah is highest, the hand of the giver next, and the hand of the beggar lowest. In an addition, the hand of the beggar remains lowest until the Day of Resurrection, so abstain from asking as much as you can; if you are given something of good, let it be seen upon you, begin with those you support, give from surplus, and there is no blame upon abstinence. > **""Hands are three: the Hand of Allah is highest, the hand of the giver next, and the hand of the beggar lowest.""** > **""The hand of the beggar remains lowest until the Day of Resurrection, so abstain from asking as much as you can. If you are given something of good, let it be seen upon you, begin with those you support, give from surplus, and there is no blame upon abstinence.""**"
"4534","Atiyyah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, the giving hand is better than the lower hand. In a longer account, he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in his people's delegation. When they entered upon the Prophet ﷺ, he asked whether anyone had come with them besides them. They said, yes, a youth we left with our mounts. He said, send for him. When I was admitted while they were with him, he welcomed me and said, the giving hand is upper and the begging hand is lower; while independent do not ask, for the wealth of Allah is asked and given. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke to me in my own dialect. > **""The giving hand is better than the lower hand.""** > **""Has anyone come with you besides you. Send for him. Indeed, the giving hand is upper, and the begging hand is lower, and while independent do not ask, for the wealth of Allah is asked and given.""**"
"4535","Abu Rimthah رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was preaching, saying: > **""The hand of the giver is upper: your mother and your father, your sister and your brother, then nearest, then nearest.""**"
"4536","A man of Banu Yarbu' reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and heard him say: > **""The hand of the giver is upper: your mother and your father, your sister and your brother, then nearest, then nearest.""**"
"4537","Tha'labah ibn Zahdam al-Yarbu'i رضي الله عنه reported that he reached the Prophet ﷺ and heard him say: > **""The hand of the giver is upper: your mother and your father, your sister then your brother, and nearest, nearest.""**"
"4538","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The upper hand is better than the lower hand, and begin with those you support.""**"
"4539","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The upper hand is better than the lower hand, and begin with those you support.""**"
"4540","Rafi' ibn Khadij رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The hand of the giver is upper, and the hand of the taker is lower until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4541","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best charity is what leaves sufficiency, and the upper hand is better than the lower hand, and begin with those you support.""**"
"4542","Imran رضي الله عنه and Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The upper hand is better than the lower hand, and begin with those you support: your mother and your father, and nearest, nearest.""**"
"4543","Hakim ibn Hizam رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The upper hand is better than the lower hand, and let each of you begin with those he supports, and the best charity is from sufficiency, and whoever abstains, Allah will keep him chaste, and whoever seeks independence, Allah Almighty will enrich him.""**"
"4544","Adi al-Judhami رضي الله عنه reported that he met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his journeys and said, O Messenger of Allah, I had two wives, they fought, so I struck one and killed her. He said, pay her blood-money and do not inherit her. It is as if I see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon a red jad'a she-camel, saying: > **""Pay her blood-money and do not inherit her.""** > **""O people, learn, hands are three: the Hand of Allah is highest, the hand of the giver middle, and the hand of the receiver lowest, so abstain even with bundles of firewood. Have I not conveyed. Have I not conveyed.""**"
"4545","A'ishah رضي الله عنها reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Indeed, the world is sweet and green; whoever We give thereof something without goodwill, it will be unblessed for him therein.""**"
"4546","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, this wealth is green and sweet, so whoever takes it, it will be blessed for him therein, and many a one plunges into the wealth of Allah and His Messenger in what his soul desires, for him is the Fire on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4547","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, this wealth is green and sweet.""**"
"4548","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, the world is sweet and green, so whoever takes it with its right, it will be blessed for him therein, and many a one plunges into what his soul desires, and he will have nothing on the Day of Resurrection except the Fire.""**"
"4549","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the case of the beggar who is turned away repeatedly. > **""When you have turned away the beggar three times, there is no blame upon you if you then rebuke him sternly.""**"
"4550","Muawiyah ibn Haydah (رضي الله عنه) said he asked about people seeking one another's wealth, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained the lawful occasion and its limit. > **""A man may ask on account of a sweeping calamity or a heavy liability he must discharge on behalf of his people, then when he reaches sufficiency or nears it, he must refrain.""**"
"4551","Mujahid reported that a man came to al-Hasan (رضي الله عنه) and al-Husayn (رضي الله عنه) and asked them for something, and they told him begging is lawful only in three cases: a ruinous calamity, a crushing burden of surety, or an overwhelming debt, then they gave him. He then went to Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما), who gave him without questioning him. The man remarked that his two cousins had questioned him while Ibn Umar had not, so Ibn Umar replied that the two sons of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were fed knowledge in morsels from infancy."
"4552","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the well-to-do from begging except in two ties. > **""Begging is not lawful for a rich man, except from a kinsman or from the ruler.""**"
"4553","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the world and the condition for blessing in what is received. > **""This world is green and sweet. Whoever is given something from it with contentment of soul and without greed, blessing is placed for him in it. And whoever is given something from it without our contentment and with greed from him, no blessing is placed for him in it.""**"
"4554","Al-Muttalib ibn Hantab reported that Abdullah ibn Amir sent provision and clothing to Aishah (رضي الله عنها), and she first told the messenger she accepts nothing from anyone. When the messenger departed she called him back, recalling a statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed to her. > **""O Aishah, whatever gift comes to you without asking, accept it, for it is only provision that Allah has presented to you.""**"
"4555","Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) said he reminded the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he had told him it is better for him to ask nothing from people, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ clarified the scope of that instruction. > **""That applies only to asking. Whatever Allah brings to you without asking is only sustenance that Allah has granted you.""**"
"4556","Khalid ibn Adi al-Juhani (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command acceptance of unsolicited kindness. > **""Whoever receives a kindness from his brother without asking and without greed of soul, let him accept it and not reject it, for it is only provision that Allah Almighty has driven to him.""**"
"4557","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning wealth that arrives without request. > **""Whoever is given something of this wealth without asking for it, let him accept it, for it is only provision that Allah has driven to him.""**"
"4558","Abu al-Darda (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the wealth of the ruler and explained when to take it, with al-Hasan adding that there is no harm so long as one does not journey after it or hanker for it. In another wording the Prophet ﷺ permitted eating what Allah gives without asking. > **""Whatever Allah brings to you thereof without asking and without greed, take it and increase your wealth thereby.""**"
"4559","Aidh ibn Amr (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning provision that presents itself without asking or greed, that one should use it expansively and pass it to one more needy if independent of it. Abdullah ibn Ahmad asked his father about greed, and he explained it as telling oneself that so-and-so will send something. > **""Whoever has some of this provision present itself to him without asking and without greed, let him expand his livelihood through it, and if he is independent of it, let him direct it to one who is more needy of it than he.""**"
"4560","Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command acceptance of a brother's unsought favor. > **""Whoever receives a kindness from his brother without asking and without greed, let him accept it and not reject it, for it is only provision that Allah has driven to him.""**"
"4561","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ weighed the giver in ease against the taker in need. > **""The one who gives out of plenty is not superior to the one who takes when he is needy.""**"
"4562","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ weighed the reward of the giver against the needy receiver. > **""The one who gives out of plenty does not have greater reward than the one who accepts when he is needy.""**"
"4563","Al-Hirmas ibn Ziyad (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed the right of the beggar in every state. > **""The beggar has a right, even if he comes on horseback.""**"
"4564","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade withholding from the beggar on account of apparent wealth. > **""Let none of you refrain from giving the beggar, even if he sees upon his hands two bangles of gold.""**"
"4565","Anas (رضي الله عنه) described two beggars who came to the Prophet ﷺ. For each he ordered a single date. The first took it with aversion, while the second received it with gratitude, exclaiming wonder at a date from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ sent provision to him. > **""SubhanAllah, a date from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ! Go to Umm Salamah and give him the forty dirhams that are with her.""**"
"4566","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked rejection of the poor to their untruthfulness, in two wordings: were it not that the poor lie, none who turns them away would prosper, and were the poor truthful, none who turns them away would prosper. > **""Were it not that the poor lie, none who turns them away would prosper. Were the poor truthful, none who turns them away would prosper.""**"
"4567","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to narrate to them about al-Khidr (عليه السلام), and they accepted, whereupon he narrated the account that follows. > **""Shall I not narrate to you about al-Khidr? Once while he was walking in the market of the Children of Israel, a mukatab saw him and said: Give me charity, may Allah bless you. Al-Khidr said: I believe in Allah, whatever Allah wills comes to be, I have nothing to give you. The poor man said: I ask you by the Face of Allah to give me charity, for I see generosity in your face and I hope for blessing with you. Al-Khidr said: I believe in Allah, I have nothing to give you, unless you take me and sell me. The poor man said: Can this be right? He said: Yes, I speak the truth. You have asked me by a mighty matter, and I will not disappoint one who asks me by my Lord. Sell me. He brought him to the market and sold him for four hundred dirhams. He remained with the buyer a time without being set to any work, so he said to him: You bought me only seeking some good from me, so direct me to some work. The buyer said: I dislike burdening you, for you are an aged weak elder. He said: It will not burden me. He said: Then rise and move these stones, which six men could not move in a day. The buyer left for a need, then returned to find the stones moved within an hour, and said: You have done well and nobly, and borne what I did not think you could bear. Then a journey came upon the buyer, so he said: I deem you trustworthy, so deputize for me among my family with good deputyship, and al-Khidr asked for work. The buyer said he disliked burdening him, he replied it would not burden him, so the buyer told him to mold bricks for his house until his return. The buyer departed, then returned to find the building raised. He said: I ask you by the Face of Allah, what is your way and your affair? He said: You asked me by the Face of Allah, and the Face of Allah brought me into bondage. I shall tell you who I am. I am al-Khidr of whom you have heard. A poor man asked me for charity when I had nothing, then he asked me by the Face of Allah, so I offered him my neck and he sold me. And I tell you: whoever is asked by the Face of Allah and turns away his asker while able, will stand on the Day of Resurrection as mere skin, with no flesh and no bone, rattling. The buyer said: I believe in Allah, I burdened you, O Prophet of Allah, without knowing. He said: No harm, you did well and were Godfearing. The buyer said: By my father and mother, O Prophet of Allah, decree concerning my family and wealth as you wish, or choose and I shall free you. He said: I love that you free me so I may worship my Lord. So he freed him, and al-Khidr said: Praise be to Allah who brought me into bondage and then delivered me from it.""**"
"4568","Abu Ubayd, freedman of Rifaah ibn Rafi (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced a curse upon both sides of asking by Allah. > **""Cursed is he who asks by the Face of Allah, and cursed is he who is asked by the Face of Allah and then withholds from his asker.""**"
"4569","Abu Musa al-Ashari (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounce a curse with an exception for an indecent request. > **""Cursed is he who asks by the Face of Allah, and cursed is he who is asked by the Face of Allah and then withholds from his asker, unless he asks for something foul.""**"
"4570","Abdullah ibn Abdurrahman reported that Umar (رضي الله عنه) came to al-Jabiyah of Damascus and announced that when he leaves his station none entitled to charity should remain without coming to him. None of those present came except two men, for whom he ordered and they were given. A man stood and said the self-sufficient hoarder has no more right to charity than the abstinent poor, whereupon Umar said: Woe to you, how shall we find those others."
"4571","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man would come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and accept Islam for some worldly gain, seeking nothing thereby but that gain, yet evening would not come before Islam had become dearer to him than the world and all it contains."
"4572","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported in another wording that a man would ask the Prophet ﷺ for some worldly thing and accept Islam on that account, and the rest is with the same meaning as the preceding narration."
"4573","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said every Muslim owes a prayer of charity each morning upon his person. A man said this is severe and who can bear it, whereupon he explained its forms. > **""Enjoining good is a prayer, forbidding evil is a prayer, carrying a load for the weak is a prayer, and every step one of you takes toward prayer is a prayer.""**"
"4574","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported in another wording that every Muslim of the children of Adam owes a charity each day, with prayer replaced by charity, and that the two rakas of Duha suffice for all of that. > **""Each morning there is a charity due upon every Muslim of the children of Adam each day, and the two rakas of Duha suffice for all of that.""**"
"4575","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ imposed a daily charity upon every Muslim. A man asked who can bear that, whereupon he enumerated its forms. > **""Your removing harm from the road is charity, your guiding a man on the road is charity, your forbidding evil is charity, your visiting the sick is charity, your following the funeral is charity, and a Muslim returning the greeting of peace to a Muslim is charity.""**"
"4576","A companion reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said man has three hundred and sixty bones, or thirty-six joints, upon each of which charity is due daily. When asked about one who lacks means, he graded alternatives from enjoining good to withholding one's evil from people. > **""Man has three hundred and sixty bones, upon him is a charity each day. Enjoin good and forbid evil. Whoever cannot, let him lift a bone from the road. Whoever cannot, let him guide to the way. Whoever cannot, let him aid the weak. Whoever cannot, let him withhold people from his evil.""**"
"4577","Abdurrahman ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore by Him in whose Hand is his soul upon three matters: wealth is not diminished by charity so give charity, no servant pardons an injustice seeking thereby the Face of Allah but Allah increases him in honor on the Day of Resurrection, and no servant opens a door of begging but Allah opens upon him a door of poverty. > **""Wealth is never diminished by charity, so give charity. No servant pardons an injustice seeking thereby the Face of Allah but Allah increases him thereby in honor on the Day of Resurrection. And no servant opens a door of begging but Allah opens upon him a door of poverty.""**"
"4578","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked pardon for Allah's sake to increased honor, and charity to increase, not decrease. > **""No one is wronged by an injustice and then leaves it for Allah but Allah increases him thereby in honor. And give charity, for charity does not decrease wealth, but increases it therein.""**"
"4579","Umm Salamah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said charity does not lessen wealth, pardon increases honor, and begging opens poverty. > **""Wealth is not lessened by charity. No man pardons an injustice but Allah increases him thereby in honor, so pardon and Allah will honor you. And no man opens upon himself a door of begging but Allah opens upon him a door of poverty.""**"
"4580","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded self-protection from the Fire. > **""Let each of you shield his face from the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4581","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded guarding against the Fire. > **""Guard against the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4582","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported in another wording that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed her concerning the sufficiency of even half a date for the hungry and the full. > **""O Aishah, shield yourself from the Fire, even with half a date, for it fills the need of the hungry as it fills that of the full.""**"
"4583","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon the timbers of the minbar describe the effects of even half a date. > **""Guard against the Fire, even with half a date, for it straightens crookedness, repels an evil death, and suffices the hungry as it suffices the full.""**"
"4584","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the command of protection through charity. > **""Guard against the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4585","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded protection from the Fire. > **""Guard against the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4586","Numan ibn Bashir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded guarding against the Fire. > **""Guard against the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4587","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded guarding against the Fire. > **""Guard against the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4588","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported the preceding narration with the addition of an address to Aishah (رضي الله عنها) commanding her to ransom herself and never to return a beggar empty. > **""O Aishah, ransom yourself from Allah. I shall avail you nothing against Allah, even with half a date. O Aishah, let no beggar return from you, even with a burnt hoof.""**"
"4589","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people with the command of protection. > **""O people, guard against the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4590","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded charity as ransom. > **""Give charity, for charity is your ransom from the Fire.""**"
"4591","Abdullah ibn Abdurrahman reported he heard Abdullah ibn Mujammir of Yemen narrate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Aishah (رضي الله عنها) to seek protection. > **""Seek protection from the Fire, even with half a date.""**"
"4592","Fadalah ibn Ubayd (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the command to place a barrier. > **""Place between yourselves and the Fire a veil, even with half a date.""**"
"4593","Abu Juhayfah (رضي الله عنه) described destitute people of Qays in striped cloaks with swords slung, whose state grieved the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He prayed, entered his house, returned, prayed, sat in his place, then ordered charity and urged it upon them. > **""Let a man give charity from his dinar, let him give from his dirham, let a man give from his sa of wheat, let a man give from his sa of dates.""**"
"4594","Adi ibn Hatim (رضي الله عنه) described Bedouins coming to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at midday with swords slung and mean garments, whereupon he urged the people concerning them to spend from every kind before a Day when only Fire will be seen in every direction. > **""Let the owner of dinars spend from his dinars, and the owner of dirhams from his dirhams, and the owner of wheat from his wheat, and the owner of barley from his barley, and the owner of dates from his dates, before there comes upon him a Day when he looks before him and sees only the Fire, and looks to his right and sees only the Fire, and looks to his left and sees only the Fire, and looks behind him and sees only the Fire.""**"
"4595","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the due right of camels and defined it by sacrifice, lending the stallion, and milking on the day of watering. > **""That he sacrifice its fat one, and lend its stallion for covering, and milk it on the day it is brought to water.""**"
"4596","Al-Sharid (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking about camels, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised Paradise in peace for fulfilling its right. > **""Sacrifice its fat one, mount upon its noble one, and milk on the day of its watering, you shall enter Paradise in peace.""**"
"4597","Qays ibn Asim al-Minqari (رضي الله عنه) came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who greeted him as the chief of the people of the desert. He asked about wealth free of liability toward guest and large family, was told of forty and sixty and the woe of hundreds except for the liberal giver, praised those traits, described his valley crowded with camels open to all takers and his lending of backs except the young and the aged dam, was asked whether his own wealth or his heirs' is dearer, answered his own, was told one's true wealth is what one eats to end, wears to wear out, or gives to send forth, and vowed to diminish their number. Al-Hasan (رحمه الله) said he did so. At death Qays enjoined his sons to make the eldest chief and not the youngest lest people deem them foolish, to tend wealth as the pride of the noble and independence from the base, to avoid begging as the last earning of a man that abandons earning, to wail not over him since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade wailing, to shroud him in his prayer and fasting garments, and to bury him hidden for fear of Bakr ibn Wail violating the grave from pre-Islamic feud lest their religion and world be harmed. Al-Hasan said he counseled them in life and in death. > **""This is the chief of the people of the desert. Excellent wealth is forty, and more is sixty, and woe to the owners of hundreds, except one who gives in its milk and its hardship, and lends its back, and sacrifices its fat, feeding the content and the supplicant. And how do you manage? What do you do with lending the back? Is your wealth dearer to you or the wealth of your freedmen? You have of your wealth only what you eat and finish, or wear and wear out, or give and send forth, otherwise it belongs to your heirs.""**"
"4598","Yazid ibn al-Akhnas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ confined worthy rivalry to two men, whereupon a man asked about valor in a man, but the remainder of the narration is missing. > **""There is no rivalry among you except toward two: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, who stands with it by night and day and follows what is in it, so a man says: Had Allah given me like what He gave so-and-so, I would stand with it as he stands. And a man to whom Allah has given wealth, which he spends and gives in charity, so a man says: Had Allah given me like what He gave so-and-so, I would give charity therewith.""**"
"4599","Abu Said al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ confined envy to two types. > **""There is no envy except toward two: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, which he recites by night and by day, so a man hears him and says: Would that I were given like what this one was given, that I might act therein as he acts. And a man to whom Allah has given wealth, which he expends in truth, so a man says: Would that I were given like what this one was given, that I might act therein as he acts.""**"
"4600","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ confined envy to two men defined by adherence and liberality. > **""Envy is only toward two: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, who upholds it, declaring its lawful lawful and its unlawful unlawful, and a man to whom Allah has given wealth, with which he joins his kin and shows mercy and acts in obedience to Allah.""**"
"4601","Buraydah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the struggle in every charitable outlay. > **""No man brings forth anything as charity until he has wrested it from the jaws of seventy devils.""**"
"4602","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) described a sheep ordered to be slaughtered and divided among neighbors. He slaughtered and distributed it, while the foreleg, the Prophet's most beloved part, was raised to him. When the Prophet ﷺ came, Aishah (رضي الله عنها) said only the foreleg remained with them, whereupon he corrected her reckoning. > **""All of it remains except the foreleg.""**"
"4603","Abu Dharr (رضي الله عنه) asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about prayer, the merit of charity, the best deed in his soul being fasting, and then graded forms of charity from surplus food down to withholding evil, with the Prophet ﷺ warning against leaving no good in oneself. > **""Prayer is the perfection of action. Charity is a wondrous thing. It is good, but that is not it here. With the surplus of your food. With half a date. With a good word. Withhold people from evil, for it is a charity with which you give charity upon yourself. Do you wish to leave no good in you at all?""**"
"4604","Rafi ibn Khadij (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the protective effect of charity. > **""Charity closes seventy doors of evil.""**"
"4605","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning people who passed by Isa ibn Maryam (عليه السلام). He foretold the death of one of them that day, they returned in the evening with bundles of firewood, he ordered them set down and the foretold one to untie his bundle, wherein was a black snake, and upon questioning the man recalled giving part of a loaf in his hand to a beggar, whereupon Isa explained the aversion. > **""One of these shall die today if Allah wills. Put them down. Untie your bundle. What did you do today? Look what you did. By it was averted from you.""**"
"4606","Ali ibn Abi Talib (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded early charity for its barrier against affliction. > **""Give charity early in the morning, for affliction does not leap over it.""**"
"4607","Abdullah ibn Jafar (رضي الله عنهما) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the effect of charity. > **""Charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord.""**"
"4608","Rafi ibn Makith (رضي الله عنه), of those present at al-Hudaybiyah, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked good mastery to growth, bad character to misfortune, righteousness to lengthened life, and charity to protection. > **""Good management of those under authority is growth, and evil character is misfortune, and righteousness is increase in life, and charity guards against an evil death.""**"
"4609","Amr ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the effects of the Muslim's charity. > **""The charity of the Muslim increases life, prevents an evil death, and Allah removes thereby pride, poverty, and vainglory.""**"
"4610","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ singled out the best door of goodness. > **""The best door of righteousness is charity.""**"
"4611","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) raised to the Prophet ﷺ the statement that charity never decreases wealth, that the hand extended with charity meets the Hand of Allah before the beggar's hand, and that opening begging while independent opens poverty. > **""Charity never decreases wealth. No servant extends his hand with charity but it is placed in the Hand of Allah before it falls in the hand of the beggar. And no servant opens a door of begging while independent thereof but Allah opens upon him a door of poverty.""**"
"4612","Uqbah ibn Amir (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise shade until judgment. > **""Every person shall be in the shade of his charity until judgment is passed among people.""**"
"4613","A companion of the Prophet ﷺ reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the believer's shade on the Resurrection Day is his charity. The narrator added that Yazid would not let a day pass without giving something, even a cake or an onion or the like. > **""The shade of the believer on the Day of Resurrection is his charity.""**"
"4614","Uqbah ibn Amir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described charity cooling the grave and shading the believer. > **""Charity extinguishes from its people the heat of the graves, and the believer shall only take shade on the Day of Resurrection in the shade of his charity.""**"
"4615","Abu Barzah al-Aslami (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the growth of even a morsel. > **""The servant gives a morsel in charity, and it grows with Allah Almighty until it becomes like Uhud.""**"
"4616","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Prophet ﷺ described Allah's acceptance and nurturing of charity. > **""Allah accepts charity and nurtures it for one of you as one of you nurtures his foal or his weaned young camel.""**"
"4617","Maymunah bint Sad (رضي الله عنها) said she asked for a ruling on charity, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described its condition and effect for the sincere seeker of Allah's Face. > **""It is a veil from the Fire for one who anticipates reward therein seeking thereby the Face of Allah Almighty.""**"
"4618","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) said charity falls in the Hand of Allah Almighty before it falls in the hand of the beggar, then he recited the verse: And He it is who accepts repentance from His servants."
"4619","Ali ibn Abi Talib (رضي الله عنه) described three men who came to the Prophet ﷺ. One owned a hundred dinars and gave ten in charity, another owned ten dinars and gave one, and the third owned a single dinar and gave a tenth of it, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared their equality. > **""All of you are equal in reward. Each of you has given charity with a tenth of his wealth.""**"
"4620","Abu Malik al-Ashari (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described three men: one with ten dinars giving one, another with ten uqiyyahs giving one, and another with a hundred uqiyyahs giving ten, declaring them equal and reciting the verse: Let the owner of plenty spend from his plenty. > **""They are equal in reward. Each has given a tenth of his wealth. Let the owner of plenty spend from his plenty.""**"
"4621","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that every doer of good, Muslim or disbeliever, is rewarded. When asked about the disbeliever, he explained worldly increase and lightened punishment in the Hereafter, and recited: Admit the folk of Pharaoh into the severest punishment. > **""No doer of good among Muslim or disbeliever does good but he is rewarded. When he gives charity, or joins kin, or does a good deed, Allah rewards him, and his reward is wealth and children in this world, and a punishment short of the full punishment in the Hereafter.""**"
"4622","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged archery and contest, promised Paradise for one arrow to its maker anticipating reward, its supplier, and its shooter, and promised Paradise to three for a morsel benefiting the poor, then praised Allah for forgetting none. > **""Shoot and compete, and that you compete is dearer to me. Allah Almighty admits by a single arrow into Paradise its maker who anticipates reward therein, its supplier, and its shooter. And Allah Almighty admits by a morsel of bread, a handful of dates, and the like by which the poor benefit, three into Paradise: the master of the house who commands it, the wife who prepares it, and the servant who hands it to the poor. Praise be to Allah who has not forgotten any of us.""**"
"4623","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) described the revelation of the verse of the grain growing seven ears with a hundred grains in each, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for increase, then the verse of the goodly loan multiplied many times was revealed and he supplicated again, then the verse of the patient paid without reckoning was revealed. > **""My Lord, increase my nation. My Lord, increase my nation.""**"
"4624","Uthman reported that Harithah (رضي الله عنه) lost his sight and fixed a cord from his prayer place to the door of his chamber, placing beside it a basket of dates and other things. When a poor man came and greeted, he would take from the basket and follow the cord to hand it over himself. His family offered to suffice him in this, and he cited what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Handing to the poor guards against an evil death.""**"
"4625","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning charity from wholesome earnings, Allah's acceptance being confined to the pure, the Merciful taking it in His Hand and rearing it as one rears a foal or a young servant or a weaned camel. > **""A man gives charity from wholesome earnings, and Allah accepts only the pure, so the Most Merciful takes it in His Hand and rears it as one of you rears his foal or his young servant or his weaned camel.""**"
"4626","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) raised to the Prophet ﷺ the rule concerning foul offsetting foul and pure offsetting foul. > **""The foul does not expiate the foul, but the pure expiates the foul.""**"
"4627","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) intended to give some foul-smelling meat in charity, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ questioned her about giving what she herself would not eat. > **""Would you give in charity what you would not eat?""**"
"4628","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that a mastigure was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ and he did not eat it. She asked whether it should be fed to the poor, and he forbade feeding them what they themselves would not eat. > **""Do not feed them what you do not eat.""**"
"4629","Jarir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ saw he kept no wealth but spent it all, and advised him that keeping his wealth involved no blame since the affair has its term. > **""O Jarir, there is no blame upon you to keep your wealth for yourself, for this affair has its term.""**"
"4630","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred giving his ring in charity over sending a thousand dirhams as a gift to the Kabah. > **""That I give my ring in charity is more beloved to me than a thousand dirhams I send as a gift to the Kabah.""**"
"4631","Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) reported that when the verse concerning the goodly loan to Allah was revealed, al-Dahdah asked whether their Lord had sought a loan from their wealth. The Prophet ﷺ affirmed it, and the man offered the better of his two gardens, one in the high ground and one in the low. The Prophet ﷺ assigned it to the orphan in their care and then spoke of his reward in Paradise. > **""Yes. It is for the orphan who is with you. Many a bunch hanging low in Paradise belongs to Ibn al-Dahdah.""**"
"4632","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that when the verse of the goodly loan was revealed, Abu al-Dahdah asked whether Allah seeks a loan from them. The Prophet ﷺ affirmed it addressing him by name, whereupon he declared he had loaned his garden containing six hundred palms. He then walked to the garden where Umm al-Dahdah was with her household, called her, she answered at your service, and he told her to come out for he had loaned the walled garden to his Lord. > **""Yes, O Abu al-Dahdah.""**"
"4633","Ulbah ibn Zayd (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged charity, so he stood and said he owned nothing but his honor, which he had given to whoever wronged him. The Prophet ﷺ turned away from him. On the next day he asked for him, or for the giver of his honor, and announced Allah's acceptance. > **""Where is Ulbah ibn Zayd? Or where is the giver of his honor in charity? For Allah Almighty has accepted it from him.""**"
"4634","Amr ibn Awf (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged charity one day, so Ulbah ibn Zayd stood and said he owned only his honor, calling the Prophet ﷺ to witness that he had given it to whoever wronged him, then sat down. The Prophet ﷺ asked for him two or three times until he stood, then confirmed his description and Allah's acceptance. > **""You are the giver of your honor in charity. Allah has accepted it from you.""**"
"4635","Abu Abs ibn Jabr (رضي الله عنه) reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged zakat, Ulbah ibn Zayd al-Harithi supplicated that he owned nothing to give except sticks bearing a water-skin and a pillow stuffed with fiber, and that he gave his honor to whoever assailed it. In the morning the Prophet ﷺ commanded a crier to call for the giver of his honor the night before. There was silence, the call was repeated two or three times, then Ulbah stood, and the Prophet ﷺ looked at him and announced acceptance by name. > **""Indeed Allah Almighty has accepted your charity, O Abu Muhammad.""**"
"4636","Muawiyah ibn Haydah (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the effect of charity given in secret upon the anger of the Lord Almighty. > **""Secret charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord Almighty.""**"
"4637","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked acts of good to protection from evil deaths, secret charity to extinguishing anger, and kinship to length of life. > **""Acts of good guard against evil deaths, secret charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord, and joining kinship increases life.""**"
"4638","Muhammad ibn Ali (رحمه الله) said he asked Abdullah ibn Jafar (رضي الله عنهما) to relate a hadith heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he cited the effect of secret charity upon the Lord's anger. > **""Secret charity extinguishes the anger of the Lord.""**"
"4639","Umm Salamah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked good deeds to protection, hidden charity to extinguishing anger, kinship to added life, every good to charity, the people of good and of evil to their standing in both abodes, and the people of good to first entry into Paradise. > **""Acts of good guard against evil deaths, charity in secret extinguishes the anger of the Lord, joining kinship is an increase in life, every good is charity, the people of good in this world are the people of good in the Hereafter, the people of evil in this world are the people of evil in the Hereafter, and the first to enter Paradise are the people of good.""**"
"4640","Jabir ibn Abdullah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defined the most excellent charity by continued independence, commanded beginning with dependents, and preferred the upper hand to the lower. > **""The best charity is that given after sufficiency, and begin with those you maintain, and the upper hand is better than the lower hand.""**"
"4641","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat in a gathering while they thought revelation might be descending, so they held back until Abu Dharr came forward and sat with him. The narration continues at length until he asked what charity is and which charity is best, and the Prophet ﷺ described its multiplication and increase with Allah and named its best forms. > **""Manifold multiples, and with Allah is increase. Secret giving to a poor man, and the toil of one who has little.""**"
"4642","Abu Dharr (رضي الله عنه) reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what charity is and which is best, and was told of its multiplied reward and its best forms in the effort of the straitened and concealment toward the poor. > **""Manifold multiples. The toil of one who has little, or secret giving to a poor man.""**"
"4643","Qatadah ibn Sad (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ about the best prayer, the best charity, and the most complete believers, and was told of length of standing, the effort of the straitened, and excellence of character. > **""Length of standing. The toil of one who has little. The best of them in character.""**"
"4644","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that Abu Dharr asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what charity is, and he described its multiplied reward and increase with Allah, then recited the verse of the goodly loan multiplied many times. Asked which charity is best, he named hidden giving and the effort of the straitened, then recited the verse concerning open and hidden charities. > **""Manifold multiples, and with Allah is increase. Who is it that will loan Allah a goodly loan so He multiplies it for him many times over. Secret giving to a poor man, or the toil of one who has little. If you disclose charities, how excellent they are.""**"
"4645","Hakim ibn Hizam (رضي الله عنه) reported that he asked the Prophet ﷺ which charity is best, and was commanded to begin with dependents and to give while remaining independent. > **""Begin with those you maintain, and charity given after sufficiency.""**"
"4646","Al-Hakam ibn Umayr (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the most beloved deeds to Allah Almighty in feeding the hungry poor, settling debt, and lifting distress. > **""The most beloved deeds to Allah Almighty are that one feed a poor man suffering hunger, or settle a debt upon him, or lift a distress from him.""**"
"4647","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ singled out charity to the spiteful kinsman as most excellent. > **""The best charity is charity to the spiteful kinsman.""**"
"4648","Hakim ibn Hizam (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which charities are best, and he named giving to the spiteful kinsman. > **""To the spiteful kinsman.""**"
"4649","Abu Talhah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ distinguished charity to the poor from charity to the kinsman, the latter combining two virtues. > **""Charity to the poor is charity, and to the kinsman is charity and joining.""**"
"4650","Umm Kulthum bint Uqbah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Prophet ﷺ described the most excellent charity as that given to the spiteful kinsman. > **""The best charity is charity to the spiteful kinsman.""**"
"4651","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the reward of charity to a relative as doubled twofold. > **""Charity to a relative has its reward doubled twofold.""**"
"4652","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore by Him who sent him with truth that Allah will not punish on the Resurrection Day one who shows mercy to the orphan, speaks gently to him, pities his orphanhood and weakness, and does not vaunt over his neighbor with Allah's bounty. He then addressed his nation with an oath that Allah accepts no charity diverted from needy kin to others and will not look upon its doer. > **""By Him who sent me with the truth, Allah will not punish on the Day of Resurrection one who shows mercy to the orphan, is gentle to him in speech, pities his orphanhood and weakness, and does not vaunt over his neighbor with the surplus Allah has given him. O nation of Muhammad, by Him who sent me with the truth, Allah does not accept charity from a man whose needy kin require his joining while he diverts it to others. By Him in whose Hand is my soul, Allah will not look at him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4653","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood among men and women, urged the men to charity, then turned to the women with the same urging. Zaynab, the wife of Abdullah, sent Bilal (رضي الله عنه) asking him to convey greeting from a Muhajir woman without naming her, and to ask whether she earns reward by placing her charity with her migrant husband who owns nothing and with orphan nephews in her care. Bilal brought the question, and the Prophet ﷺ affirmed two rewards. > **""Yes. She has her reward twofold: the reward of kinship and the reward of charity.""**"
"4654","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ left the dawn prayer one day, came to the women in the mosque, stood over them and warned them concerning deficiency of mind and religion, their majority among the people of Fire, and the need to draw near to Allah as able. The wife of Abdullah ibn Masud went to him, told him what she heard, and took her jewelry intending nearness to Allah and His Messenger in hope of escape from the Fire. Ibn Masud told her to give it to him and his child as its proper place. She refused until consulting the Prophet ﷺ, sought permission, was asked which Zaynab and identified as the wife of Abdullah, was admitted, repeated the account, and was told to give it to them. She then asked about the statement on deficiency, what deficiency of religion and minds means, and was told of menses preventing prayer and fasting and of a woman's testimony being half a man's. > **""O company of women, I have not seen any deficient in mind and religion more sweeping to the hearts of the resolute than you, and I have seen that you are the majority of the people of Fire on the Day of Resurrection, so draw near to Allah as much as you can. Admit her. Give it in charity to him and to his sons, for they are its proper place. As for what I mentioned of deficiency in your religion, it is the menses that befall you, when one of you remains as long as Allah wills without praying or fasting, that is part of the deficiency of your religion. And as for what I mentioned of deficiency in your minds, it is your testimony, for a woman's testimony is only half a man's testimony.""**"
"4655","Raitah, wife of Abdullah ibn Masud and mother of his child, was skilled with her hand and spent from her craft upon him and his child. She complained to Abdullah that he and his child kept her from charity so she could give nothing alongside them. He swore he would dislike her doing so if she earned no reward therein. She came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, explained she earns by her craft with no other maintenance for herself, her child, or her husband, and asked whether spending upon them earns reward. He commanded spending and promised reward for it. > **""Spend upon them, for you have therein the reward of what you spend upon them.""**"
"4656","Abu Said al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out on Adha or Fitr, prayed, admonished the people and commanded them to give charity, then passed the women and warned them that he had seen them as the majority of the Fire. Asked why, he cited frequent cursing, ingratitude to husbands, and deficiency of mind and religion sweeping the resolute man's heart. Asked what that deficiency is, he cited a woman's testimony as half a man's, and menses preventing prayer, as deficiency of mind and of religion. He then returned home, where the wife of Abdullah ibn Masud sought permission, was asked which Zaynab and identified, was admitted, explained she had jewelry she meant for charity while Ibn Masud claimed he and his child were most deserving, and the Prophet ﷺ confirmed his truthfulness. > **""O people, give charity. Give charity, for I have seen you as the majority of the people of Fire. You curse often and deny the husband. I have not seen any deficient in mind and religion more sweeping to the heart of the resolute man than one of you, O company of women. Is not a woman's testimony half a man's testimony? That is part of the deficiency of her mind. When a woman menstruates does she not pray? That is part of the deficiency of her religion. Admit her. Ibn Masud has spoken truly. Your husband and your child are most deserving of those upon whom you bestow your charity.""**"
"4657","Jamrah bint Quhafah (رضي الله عنها) reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Farewell Pilgrimage command women to give even from their jewelry, warning they are the majority of the Fire. Zaynab then asked whether she may bestow upon her needy husband, and he affirmed two rewards. > **""O company of women, give charity even from your jewelry, for you are the majority of the people of Fire. Yes. You have two rewards.""**"
"4658","Amr ibn Umayyah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe a man's gift to his wife as charity. > **""Whatever a man gives his wife is charity.""**"
"4659","Al-Irbad ibn Sariyah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise reward for giving one's wife water. He then went to his wife, gave her to drink, and told her what he had heard. > **""When a man gives his wife water to drink, he is rewarded.""**"
"4660","Al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted feeding oneself, one's child, one's wife, and one's servant all as charity for the giver. > **""What you feed yourself is charity for you, what you feed your child is charity for you, what you feed your wife is charity for you, and what you feed your servant is charity for you.""**"
"4661","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised one who maintains two daughters, two sisters, two paternal aunts, or two maternal aunts the opening of the eight gates of Paradise, calling upon Allah's servants to aid, give, and lend him. > **""Whoever has two daughters, or two sisters, or two paternal aunts, or two maternal aunts, and maintains them, the eight gates of Paradise are opened for him. O servants of Allah, aid him. O servants of Allah, give him. O servants of Allah, lend him.""**"
"4662","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted a man's spending upon himself, his child, his family, his womb-relatives, and his kin as charity for him. > **""Whatever a man spends upon himself, his child, his family, his womb-relative, and his kin is charity for him.""**"
"4663","Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking whom to give surplus from what Allah entrusted to him, and was commanded to begin with mother, father, sister, brother, then nearest, without forgetting neighbors and the needy. > **""Begin with your mother, your father, your sister, your brother, then nearest then nearest, and do not forget neighbors and the needy.""**"
"4664","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preferred the upper hand to the lower and commanded beginning with dependents in their order of nearness. > **""The upper hand is better than the lower hand, and begin with those you maintain: your mother, your father, your sister, your brother, then nearest then nearest.""**"
"4665","Sasah ibn Najiyah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and asked about surplus he stores for calamity and the wayfarer, and was directed to mother, father, sister, brother, then nearest. > **""Your mother and your father, your sister and your brother, then nearest then nearest.""**"
"4666","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted spending upon oneself for chastity and spending upon wife, child, and household as charity. > **""Whoever spends upon himself a charity by which he preserves his chastity, it is charity, and whoever spends upon his wife, his child, and his household, it is charity.""**"
"4667","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described a man's spending upon his family as charity. > **""A man's spending upon his family is charity.""**"
"4668","Abu Said al-Khudri (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ thrice declared ruin upon the wealthy hoarders. Each time they asked who is excepted, until they feared it settled as obligation, whereupon he excepted one who gives thus and thus on all sides, noting how few they are. > **""Except one who says thus and thus and thus, and how few they are.""**"
"4669","Abu Dharr (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him what mountain this is, he answered Uhud, and the Prophet ﷺ swore he would dislike holding it as pieces of gold spent in Allah's way while leaving a qirat. Asked whether the exception is a qintar, he insisted it is a qirat, repeating it thrice, then explained he states the least, not the most. > **""O Abu Dharr, what mountain is this? By Him in whose Hand is my soul, it would not please me that it were mine as pieces of gold I spend in the way of Allah Almighty, leaving of it a qirat. A qirat. I only state what is least, and I do not state what is most.""**"
"4670","Abu al-Salil reported that a man halted at their gathering in al-Baqi and related from his father or uncle that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in al-Baqi promising his witness on the Resurrection Day for whoever gives charity. The narrator unwrapped one or two coils of his turban intending to give them, then human hesitation seized him and he retied it. Then a very dark man arrived leading a she-camel finer than any seen in al-Baqi, asked whether to give it, was told yes, and offered it. Another man nudged him saying the camel is better than him, the Prophet ﷺ heard it, thrice declared him wrong and the giver better, thrice warned owners of hundreds of camels, excepted the generous giver gesturing with both hands to right and left, then thrice declared success for the striving ascetic, ascetic in livelihood and striving in worship. > **""Whoever gives charity, I shall bear witness for him thereby on the Day of Resurrection. Yes. Take this she-camel. You have lied, rather he is better than you and than it. Woe to the owners of hundreds of camels. Except one who says thus and thus. The striving ascetic has succeeded. The ascetic in livelihood, the striving in worship.""**"
"4671","Abdurrahman ibn Abza (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared ruin upon the wealthy hoarders except one who gives on all sides. > **""The wealthy hoarders have perished, except one who says thus and thus.""**"
"4672","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Ansar heard Abu Ubaydah had arrived with wealth from Bahrain, whither the Prophet ﷺ had sent him, so they joined the dawn prayer with him. When he finished they faced him, he smiled at them, asked whether they heard of the arrival with wealth, they affirmed it, and he bade them glad tidings and hope for good, swearing his fear is not poverty but worldly rivalry as with those before them. > **""Perhaps you have heard that Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah has arrived, and has arrived with wealth? Rejoice and hope for good. By Allah, it is not poverty I fear for you, but when the world is poured upon you and you rival therein as those before you rivaled therein.""**"
"4673","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disclaimed fear of poverty and of error, and affirmed fear of rivalry for increase and of deliberate wrongdoing. > **""I do not fear poverty for you, but I fear rivalry for increase for you, and I do not fear error for you, but I fear deliberate sin for you.""**"
"4674","Abu Sinan al-Duali reported that he entered upon Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) while early Muhajirun sat with him. Umar sent for a chest brought from a fortress in Iraq containing a ring. One of his sons took it into his mouth, Umar pulled it away, then wept. Asked why he wept after conquest, dominance, and delight, he cited what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about worldly opening casting enmity and hatred until the Resurrection, and his fear thereof. > **""The world is not opened upon a people but Allah Almighty casts enmity and hatred among them until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4675","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned that the dinar and dirham destroyed those before them and, in his view, will destroy them likewise. > **""This dinar and dirham destroyed those before you, and I see them only destroying you.""**"
"4676","Abu al-Darda (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ described two angels dispatched at every sunrise at its flanks, calling to earth's folk save men and jinn to come to their Lord since little and sufficing is better than much and distracting, and two angels at every sunset calling for replacement for the spender and ruin for the withholder. > **""O people, come to your Lord, for what is little and suffices is better than what is much and distracts. O Allah, grant the spender replacement, and grant the withholder ruin.""**"
"4677","Abdurrahman ibn Abi Sabrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he and his father entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Asked whether this is his son, affirmed, asked his name, told Hubab, he forbade that name since Hubab is a devil and named him Abdurrahman. Then asked what wealth he holds, he answered various kinds from which he gives, frees, and mounts, but spending depletes it so he withholds it. The Prophet ﷺ reminded him of the angel's nightly call for spender and withholder. Asked with what to perform witr, he named the uppermost, the disbelievers, and sincerity chapters. > **""Do not name him Hubab, for Hubab is a devil, but he is Abdurrahman. Do you not know that an angel calls in heaven: O Allah, grant the spender replacement and the withholder of his wealth ruin? Perform witr with Glorify the Name of your Lord Most High, and Say O disbelievers, and Say He is Allah One.""**"
"4678","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that a beggar asked, she commanded the servant, who brought out something for him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ warned her against counting lest Allah count against her. > **""O Aishah, do not count, lest Allah Almighty count against you.""**"
"4679","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that every servant has three companions, one of them being his wealth, which tells him what he spends is his and what he withholds is not his. > **""There is no servant but he has three companions. As for one companion, he says: what you spend is yours, and what you withhold is not yours, and that is his wealth.""**"
"4680","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah resurrecting two servants granted abundant wealth and children. He asks the first by name, he answers at Your service, asks whether He increased him, he affirms it, asks how he acted with it, he answers he left it for his child fearing poverty upon them, and is told had he known he would laugh little and weep much, and what he feared has befallen them. He asks the second likewise, he answers he spent in obedience and trusted his Lord's bounty for his child after him, and is told had he known he would laugh much and weep little, and what he trusted in has befallen them. > **""O so-and-so son of so-and-so? Did I not increase for you wealth and children? How did you act with what I gave you? If you knew knowledge you would laugh little and weep much. What you feared for them has befallen them. O so-and-so son of so-and-so? Did I not increase for you wealth and children? How did you act with what I gave you? If you knew knowledge you would laugh much and weep little. What you trusted in for them has befallen them.""**"
"4681","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ came out to his Companions one day with a piece of gold in his hand, asked Abdullah ibn Umar what Muhammad would say to his Lord if he died with it, divided it before rising, and declared he would dislike his Companions holding Uhud in gold and silver then spending it while leaving a dinar. Ibn Abbas added that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was taken leaving no dinar, dirham, slave, or bondwoman, only his coat mortgaged with a Jew for thirty measures of barley from which he ate and fed his household. > **""What would Muhammad say to his Lord if he died with this in his possession? It would not please me that the Companions of Muhammad have the like of this mountain, gold and silver, then spend it in the way of Allah while leaving of it a dinar.""**"
"4682","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Prophet ﷺ turned to Uhud and swore he would dislike its transformation into gold for Muhammad's household to spend in Allah's way while dying with two dinars left, save two prepared for a debt upon him. > **""By Him in whose Hand is my soul, it would not please me that Uhud turn into gold for the household of Muhammad, which I spend in the way of Allah, then die the day I die leaving of it two dinars, except two dinars I prepare for a debt upon me.""**"
"4683","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say he enters the chamber only from fear wealth lies therein and he dies without spending it. > **""I do not enter this chamber but I enter it fearing there is wealth therein and I die without spending it.""**"
"4684","Samurah ibn Jundub (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would tell them he would dislike holding Uhud all as gold and then bequeathing it. > **""By Allah, it would not please me that Uhud were all mine as gold and then I bequeath it.""**"
"4685","Sahl ibn Sad (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had seven dinars left with Aishah. In his illness he repeatedly told her to send the gold to Ali, falling unconscious each time while she was occupied with his condition, until it was sent to Ali, who gave it in charity. On Monday night the Prophet ﷺ lay in the throes of death, and Aishah sent her lamp to one of his wives asking for some of her clarified butter for their lamp. > **""O Aishah, send the gold to Ali.""**"
"4686","Said ibn Amir ibn Hidhyam reported that Umar learned he stores nothing of need in his house, so sent him ten thousand. He took it and divided it into purses. His wife asked where he takes it, he answered to those who will profit them most therein, leaving only a little. When it was exhausted she told him to go to those friends and take from their profits. He put her off at length, then cited what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the Houri's finger scented by every soul, and that their wives are more deserving he leave them for the Houris than the reverse. > **""If a Houri displayed a finger of her fingers, every living soul would find its scent. Shall I leave them for you? Nay, by Allah, you are more deserving that I leave you for them than they for you.""**"
"4687","Malik al-Dar reported that Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) placed four hundred dinars in a purse and told a boy to take them to Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah and linger in the house to see what he does. The boy conveyed that the Commander of the Faithful bids him use them for his needs. Abu Ubaydah invoked joining and mercy upon him, called his girl, and ordered seven to one man, five to another, and five to another, until he exhausted it. The boy returned and told Umar, who had prepared the like for Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) and sent him likewise to observe. Muadh invoked mercy and joining, called his girl, and ordered sums to various houses, until his wife looked out and swore they too are poor, so with only two dinars left in the cloth he thrust them to her. The boy returned and told Umar, who rejoiced at that and said they are brothers one of another."
"4688","Amr ibn Hayyan al-Tai reported that Rafi ibn Umayrah al-Sunaysi (رضي الله عنه) fed the people of three mosques and gave them qartamah, meaning hays to drink, while owning only a single shirt worn both at night and for Friday."
"4689","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn that whoever dies leaving yellow or white will be branded therewith. > **""There is no servant who dies the day he dies leaving yellow or white but he is branded therewith.""**"
"4690","Abu Dharr (رضي الله عنه) reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn that whoever ties over gold or silver without spending it in Allah's way finds it as fire branding him on the Resurrection Day. > **""Whoever ties over gold or silver and does not spend it in the way of Allah, it shall be fire on the Day of Resurrection with which he is branded.""**"
"4691","Bilal (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to die poor rather than rich. Asked how that is for him, he was told not to store what is provided nor refuse what is asked. Asked again how, he was told it is that or the Fire. > **""O Bilal, die poor and do not die rich. What you are provided, do not store, and what you are asked, do not refuse. It is that or the Fire.""**"
"4692","Qays ibn Abi Hazim reported that they visited Sad ibn Masud in illness and he said he knows not what people say, but would that what is in his chest were fire. When he died they looked and found therein a thousand or two thousand."
"4693","Abu Umamah (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ leaving two dinars as debt upon him with no fulfillment. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ refused to pray over him and told them to pray over their companion. Abu Qatadah then stood and undertook its settlement, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose and prayed over him. > **""Pray over your companion.""**"
"4694","He also related that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ leaving two dinars, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described them as two brands. > **""Two brands.""**"
"4695","In another narration, a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with no shroud found for him. When the Prophet ﷺ was approached, he ordered them to look inside his waist-wrapper. A dinar or two dinars were found, whereupon he described them as brands. > **""Look inside his waist-wrapper. Two brands.""**"
"4696","In another narration, a man of Ahl al-Suffah died and a dinar was found in his waist-wrapper, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called it a brand. Then another died and two dinars were found in his waist-wrapper, whereupon he called them two brands. > **""A brand. Two brands.""**"
"4697","Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl reported that they entered upon Khabbab (رضي الله عنه) and saw uncovered dirhams in his house. Asked what they are, he answered he sold such-and-such an estate and will spend them, seeing none more deserving of them than himself."
"4698","Bilal (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered while he had some dates with him. Asked what they are, he answered they were stored for their winter. The Prophet ﷺ warned him about seeing their vapor in Hell. > **""What is this? Do you not fear to see its vapor in Hell?""**"
"4699","Bilal (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him for food, and he presented handfuls of dates. When he said only what he had stored for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained, the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to spend freely. > **""Feed us, O Bilal. Give us more, O Bilal. Spend, O Bilal, and do not fear any lessening from the Lord of the Throne.""**"
"4700","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon Bilal (رضي الله عنه) and saw with him a heap of dates stored for him and his guests. > **""What is this, O Bilal? Do you not fear that steam for it would rise in Hell? Spend, O Bilal, and do not fear any lessening from the Lord of the Throne.""**"
"4701","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ visited Bilal (رضي الله عنه) in illness, and Bilal brought out a heap of dates he had stored for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""O Bilal, do you not fear that steam in Hell would be appointed for you? Spend, O Bilal, and do not fear any lessening from the Lord of the Throne.""**"
"4702","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that a clan of the Ansar called Banu Salamah, the people of Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه), came, and the Prophet ﷺ asked them about their chief. They said it was Jadd ibn Qays, though they considered him miserly. > **""O Banu Salamah, who is your chief? And what disease is more diseased than miserliness?""**"
"4703","Abu Salih reported from one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to order the owner of a palm tree in his walled garden to sell it or grant it to him. The Prophet ﷺ offered the owner a palm in Paradise in exchange, but he refused. > **""Do it, and you shall have a palm in Paradise. This is the most miserly of people.""**"
"4704","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man complained that another's fruit-cluster in his garden harmed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked its owner to sell it, then to grant it, then to sell it for a cluster in Paradise, but he refused each time. > **""Sell me your cluster in the garden of so-and-so. Grant it to me. Sell it to me for a cluster in Paradise. I have not seen one more miserly than you, except one who is miserly with greeting.""**"
"4705","Abu al-Qayn (رضي الله عنه) passed by the Prophet ﷺ carrying some dates. When the Prophet ﷺ reached to take a handful to scatter before his Companions, the man clutched the edge of his cloak to his belly and chest. > **""May Allah increase you in niggardliness.""**"
"4706","Imran ibn Husayn (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Allah chose this religion for Himself, and only two qualities befit it. > **""Allah has chosen this religion for Himself. Nothing befits your religion except generosity and good character. So adorn your religion with them.""**"
"4707","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) said she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the nearness of the generous and the distance of the miser. > **""The generous is near Allah, far from the Fire, near Paradise. The miser is far from Allah, far from Paradise, far from people, near the Fire. And the ignorant generous one is more beloved to Allah Almighty than the worshipping miser.""**"
"4708","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described a house in Paradise named for generosity. > **""In Paradise is a house called the House of Generosity.""**"
"4709","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that when asked who the chief is, the Prophet ﷺ named Yusuf ibn Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim (عليهم السلام). When asked about a chief in his Ummah, he described him. > **""The chief is Yusuf ibn Yaqub ibn Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. Yes, in my Ummah it is a man given lawful wealth and granted liberality, who draws the poor near, and whose complaints among people are few.""**"
"4710","Qays ibn Sala al-Ansari (رضي الله عنه) reported that his brothers complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he squandered his wealth, for he took his share of fruit and spent it in the way of Allah and upon his companions. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ struck his chest and repeated a promise three times. Afterwards he went out in the way of Allah with a mount, and he says he is now the eldest and wealthiest of his household. > **""Spend, and Allah will spend upon you.""**"
"4711","Jabir ibn Abdillah al-Sulami (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the quarter of Banu Amr ibn Awf on Wednesday and saw fortification in properties he had not seen before. He asked the Ansar to remain after Friday until they heard his speech. On Friday they attended, he prayed then offered two voluntary rakahs at his place in the mosque, contrary to his habit of praying them at home, then faced them and reminded them that in ignorance they bore burdens, did good, and joined ties, but after Allah favoured them with Islam and Muhammad ﷺ they began to fortify. He then told them of reward in eating. So they returned and every one of them broke one or three breaches in his property, meaning they demolished walls of gardens so people could enter and eat of the fruit. > **""O company of Ansar, if when you come down for your festival, meaning Friday, you remained until you heard my speech. You were in ignorance not worshipping Allah, bearing the burdened one in your wealth, doing good, and joining ties, until Allah favoured you with Islam and with Muhammad. Now you fortify. In what the son of Adam eats is reward, and in what the bird eats is reward, and in what the beast eats is reward.""**"
"4712","Yahya ibn Abbad al-Hanzali reported that a delegation came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he questioned them, but one of them denied him. The Prophet ﷺ spared him because of generosity in him, by which Allah loves him, the word mimak meaning He loves you. > **""Were it not for generosity in you, for which Allah loves you, I would have made an example of you, O envoy of a people.""**"
"4713","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that when the verse of the shares of inheritance in Surat al-Nisa was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade habs, meaning confining property from inheritance."
"4714","Fadalah ibn Ubayd (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a prohibition of habs. > **""There is no habs.""**"
"4715","Abu Salamah Bishr ibn Bashir al-Aslami reported from his father that when the Muhajirun came to Madinah they disliked its water. A man of Banu Ghifar owned a spring called Rumah, selling a skinful for a mudd. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered him a spring in Paradise for it, but he said he and his family had nothing else and could not. When Uthman (رضي الله عنه) heard, he bought it for thirty-five thousand dirhams, then asked the Prophet ﷺ whether he would grant him the same, a spring in Paradise, if he bought it. When the Prophet ﷺ said yes, he declared he had bought it and made it for the Muslims. > **""Sell it to me for a spring in Paradise. Yes.""**"
"4716","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man of the Ansar declared all his wealth charity, so his parents became destitute and sat with the needy strangers. When they complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he returned the charity to them. Then when they died, he sent to their son ordering restoration. > **""Your son's charity is returned to you two. Return the charity, for charity is neither inherited nor taken as a life-grant.""**"
"4717","Hanzalah (رضي الله عنه) said he told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he had an orphan under his care upon whom he had given one hundred camels in charity. He saw anger in his face, and the Prophet ﷺ limited charity in number until he reached forty. > **""Charity is only five, or else ten, or else fifteen, until he reached forty.""**"
"4718","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the most excellent charity to be that given to an owned slave under a bad master. > **""There is no charity better than charity given to an owned slave in the hands of an evil master.""**"
"4719","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised forgiveness and quenching for one who cares for his Muslim brother's hunger and thirst. > **""Whoever cares for the hunger of his Muslim brother and feeds him until he is full, Allah forgives him, and gives him drink until he is quenched.""**"
"4720","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised distance of seven trenches from the Fire, five hundred years between every two, for feeding and watering a brother to satisfaction. In a variant in al-Awsat the wording mentions feeding bread. > **""Whoever feeds his brother until he satisfies him, and gives him water to drink until he quenches him, Allah distances him from the Fire by seven trenches, between every two trenches five hundred years.""**"
"4721","Umar ibn al-Khattab (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked which deed is best, and he answered with bringing joy to a believer in three forms. > **""Your bringing joy to a believer: you satisfy his hunger, or you cover his nakedness, or you fulfil for him a need.""**"
"4722","Muadh ibn Jabal (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that feeding a believer from hunger earns entry through a special gate of Paradise. > **""Whoever feeds a believer until he satisfies him from hunger, Allah admits him through a gate of Paradise which none enters but one like him.""**"
"4723","Abu Junaydah al-Fihri reported from his father from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised a gate for watering the thirsty, and all gates for combining feeding and watering. > **""Whoever waters a thirsty one and quenches him, a gate of Paradise is opened for him, and it is said to him: Enter through it. And whoever feeds a hungry one and satisfies him, and waters a thirsty one and quenches him, all the gates of Paradise are opened for him, and it is said to him: Enter through whichever you wish.""**"
"4724","Iyad ibn Marthad, or Marthad ibn Iyad, reported from a man of them that he asked the Prophet ﷺ for a deed admitting him to Paradise. The Prophet ﷺ asked him repeatedly whether either parent was alive. When he said no, he commanded watering. When asked how, he explained sufficiency with the vessel and carrying. In another wording the same command is repeated. > **""Is anyone of your parents alive? Then give water to drink. Suffice them its vessel when they are present, and carry it to them when they are absent.""**"
"4725","Asim ibn Kulayb said he heard Iyad ibn Marthad, or Marthad ibn Iyad, narrating to a man that he asked the Prophet ﷺ about a deed admitting him to Paradise. After asking thrice about his parents and being told none was alive, the Prophet ﷺ commanded watering with carrying and sufficiency. > **""Is anyone of your parents alive? Give water to drink. Carry it to them when they are absent, and suffice them it when they are present.""**"
"4726","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ saying he draws in his trough until he fills it for his camels, then another's camel comes and he waters it, asking if there is reward in that. > **""In every moist liver is reward.""**"
"4727","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called water the best charity, citing the plea of the people of the Fire to the people of Paradise in al-Araf 50. > **""The best charity is water. Have you not heard the saying of the people of the Fire when they sought aid from the people of Paradise: Pour upon us some water or of what Allah has provided you.""**"
"4728","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ for a deed by which he would enter Paradise. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether he lived in a land to which water is brought. When he said yes, he commanded buying a new skin and giving drink until it wore out. > **""Do you live in a land to which water is brought? Then buy for it a new skin, then give drink therein until you wear it out, for you will not wear it out until you have reached thereby a deed of Paradise.""**"
"4729","Kudayr al-Dabbi reported that a Bedouin asked the Prophet ﷺ for a deed drawing him near Paradise and distancing him from the Fire. When the Prophet ﷺ proposed justice in speech and giving surplus, then feeding and spreading greeting, the man said he could not. So the Prophet ﷺ asked about camels and commanded taking a camel and a skin to a household who drink water only at intervals. The Bedouin departed magnifying Allah, and his skin did not tear nor his camel perish before he was killed a martyr. > **""Have the two occupied you? Speak justice and give surplus. Feed food and spread greeting. Have you camels? Then look to a camel of your camels and a skin, then betake yourself to a household who drink water only at intervals, and water them. Perhaps your camel will not perish nor your skin tear before Paradise becomes due for you.""**"
"4730","Sad ibn Ubadah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ offered to guide him to a charity light in burden and great in reward. When he accepted, the Prophet ﷺ named it, so Sad gave water. > **""O Sad, shall I not guide you to a charity light in its burden, great in its reward? Giving water to drink.""**"
"4731","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ that two men, a worshipper and another laden with sin, crossed a desert. The worshipper thirsted until he fell, while his companion had a small vessel with some water. The companion reasoned that if the righteous servant died thirsty while he had water he would never attain good from Allah, yet if he gave his water he would die. So he relied upon Allah and resolved, sprinkled some upon him and gave him the remainder, and they crossed the desert. On the Day of Resurrection the sin-laden one is brought for reckoning and ordered to the Fire. As the angels drive him he sees the worshipper and reminds him that he had preferred him over himself in the desert. The worshipper acknowledges him, tells the angels to halt, then supplicates his Lord, who grants his request and gives the man to him, so he takes his brother's hand and admits him to Paradise. Jafar ibn Abi Sulayman asked Abu Zilal whether Anas had narrated it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said yes. > **""By Allah, if this righteous servant dies thirsty while I have water, I shall never attain good from Allah. If I give him my water, I shall die. O Lord, You know his hand with me and how he preferred me over himself. O Lord, grant him to me. He is yours.""**"
"4732","Aishah (رضي الله عنها) asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what thing it is not lawful to withhold. He named three. When she said water is understood but asked about salt and fire, he explained the reward of each, and of giving a Muslim a drink where water is found or where it is not. > **""Water, salt, and fire. Whoever gives salt, it is as if he gave charity of all that the salt seasoned. Whoever gives fire, it is as if he gave charity of all that the fire ripened. Whoever gives a Muslim a drink of water where water is found, it is as if he freed a neck. And whoever gives a Muslim a drink of water where water is not found, it is as if he gave him life.""**"
"4733","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the wives of the Prophet ﷺ would go out early with skins, giving drink to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"4734","Abdullah ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked whether they knew which charity is best. When they said Allah and His Messenger know best, he named the loaned grant in four forms, with Abu Yala adding the dinar and the cow. > **""Do you know which charity is best? The grant: that one of you grants his brother the dirham, or the back of the beast, or the milk of the sheep, or the milk of the cow.""**"
"4735","Numan ibn Bashir (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise the rank of freeing a neck for grants of silver, gold, milk for drink, or a large gift. > **""Whoever grants a grant of silver, or gold, or a skin of milk, or presents a large gift, it is like freeing a neck.""**"
"4736","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) said the best charity is the loaned milch animal, going out in the morning with reward and returning in the evening with reward, the grant of a she-camel being like freeing a noble red one and the grant of a sheep like freeing a black one."
"4737","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ spoke of forty traits by which Allah admits to Paradise, the highest being the grant of a sheep. > **""Forty traits by which Allah admits to Paradise, the highest of them the grant of a sheep.""**"
"4738","Abu Dharr (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ told him to retain his words, that a young goat reaching a Muslim is better than Uhud in gold left behind. > **""O Abu Dharr, retain what I say to you. A young goat that reaches a man of the Muslims is better for him than Uhud in gold he leaves behind him.""**"
"4739","Muadh ibn Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that building or planting without injustice or aggression earns a running reward while the creation of the Most Merciful benefits thereby. > **""Whoever builds a building without injustice or aggression, or plants a planting without injustice or aggression, has a running reward while any of the creation of the Most Merciful Blessed and Exalted benefits thereby.""**"
"4740","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised reward for every eater of what a Muslim plants or sows. > **""No Muslim plants a planting, nor sows a crop, then a man eats thereof, nor a bird, nor anything, but he has reward therein.""**"
"4741","Abu Hurayrah (رضي الله عنه) reported that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ which deed is best. He answered faith and jihad, then helping a worker or doing for one unable, then restraining oneself from evil as charity for oneself. > **""Which deed is best? Faith in Allah and striving in the way of Allah. Help a maker, or do for one unable. Restrain yourself from evil, for it is a charity you give for yourself.""**"
"4742","Anas (رضي الله عنه) said the Prophet of Allah ﷺ narrated a hadith over which they rejoiced as over nothing since knowing Islam: the believer is rewarded for removing harm from the road, guiding the way, expressing for one impeded in speech, granting milk, even for goods bundled in his garment which he touches and misses with his hand. In al-Bazzar's addition he is rewarded for coming to his wife, and even for goods at the edge of his garment which he touches and ties its place, then his heart flutters for it, so Allah returns it to him and writes its reward for him. > **""The believer is rewarded for his removing harm from the road, and for his guiding the way, and for his expressing for the impeded in speech, and for the grant of milk, until he is rewarded for goods bundled in his garment which he touches and his hand misses them.""**"
"4743","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counted smiling, pouring water, removing harm, enjoining good, forbidding evil, and guiding the lost as charity. > **""Your smiling in the face of your brother is written for you as charity, and your pouring from your bucket into your brother's bucket is written for you as charity, and your removing harm from the road is written for you as charity. Your commanding good is charity, your forbidding evil is charity, and your guiding the lost is written for you as charity.""**"
"4744","Abu Dharr (رضي الله عنه) reported that he asked what saves the servant from the Fire. The Prophet ﷺ answered faith, then giving from provision, then commanding good and forbidding evil, then working for one unable, then aiding the overpowered, then refraining from harming people. When asked whether doing that admits to Paradise, he promised that any of these traits takes the Muslim's hand until it admits him to Paradise. > **""What saves the servant from the Fire? Faith in Allah. He gives from what Allah has provided him. He commands good and forbids evil. He works for one unable. He aids the overpowered. He withholds from harming people. There is no Muslim who does one of these traits but it takes his hand until it admits him to Paradise.""**"
"4745","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that a tree harmed people, so a man came and removed it from people's path. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said he saw him in its shade in Paradise. > **""I saw him tossing about in its shade in Paradise.""**"
"4746","Abu al-Darda (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ that removing harm from the Muslims' path earns a good deed, and such a deed earns Paradise. In the wording of al-Kabir the reward is one hundred good deeds. > **""Whoever removes from the path of the Muslims something that harms them, Allah writes for him thereby a good deed, and whoever has a good deed written for him with Him, He admits him thereby to Paradise.""**"
"4747","Abu Shaybah al-Mahri said Muadh (رضي الله عنه) was walking with a man and lifted a stone from the road. When asked why, he cited what he heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about lifting a stone and entering Paradise through a good deed. > **""Whoever lifts a stone from the road, a good deed is written for him, and whoever has a good deed enters Paradise.""**"
"4748","Mustanir ibn Akhdar ibn Muawiyah reported from his father that he was with Maqil ibn Yasar (رضي الله عنه) on a road. When Maqil removed harm from the road, he did likewise with another, so Maqil took his hand and asked his motive. When he said he had imitated him, Maqil cited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on removing harm and accepted deeds. > **""Whoever removes harm from the path of the Muslims, a good deed is written for him, and whoever has a good deed accepted from him enters Paradise.""**"
"4749","Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a comprehensive rule of kindness. > **""Every good is charity.""**"
"4750","Jabir ibn Abdillah (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the same comprehensive rule, adding cheerful meeting and pouring water. Here ends the narration of Imam Ahmad. > **""Every good is charity, and of good is that you meet your brother with a cheerful face, and that you pour from your bucket into his vessel.""**"
"4751","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported with Abu Yala that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared every good done to rich or poor as charity on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Every good you do to a rich or a poor one is for you charity on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4752","Jabir (رضي الله عنه) reported in another wording with Abu Yala from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that every good is charity, spending on family and wealth is charity, guarding honour thereby is charity, and every spending of the believer not in disobedience is replaced under Allah's guarantee except spending in building. Miswar said Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir told that they asked Jabir what guarding honour means, and he said giving to the poet and the foul-tongued, meaning one whose tongue is feared. > **""Every good is charity. What a man spends upon his family and his wealth is written for him as charity, and what he guards thereby his honour is for him charity. And every spending of a believer not in disobedience, upon Allah is its replacement as guarantor, except spending in building.""**"
"4753","Nubayt ibn Sharit (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ give the comprehensive rule of kindness. > **""Every good is charity.""**"
"4754","Ibn Masud (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Prophet ﷺ the same comprehensive rule, whether to rich or poor. > **""Every good is charity, whether to a rich or a poor one.""**"
"4755","Abu Masud al-Ansari (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the comprehensive rule of kindness. > **""Every good is charity.""**"
"4756","Adi ibn Thabit reported from his father from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the comprehensive rule of kindness. > **""Every good is charity.""**"
"4757","Abu Malik al-Ashjai reported from his father that the Prophet ﷺ gave the comprehensive rule of kindness. > **""Every good is charity.""**"
"4758","Abu Umamah al-Bahili (رضي الله عنه) reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ four whose rewards run after death: the frontier guard, the doer whose like is continued, the giver whose charity flows while it flows, and the leaver of a righteous child supplicating for him. > **""Four upon whom their rewards run after death: a man stationed in the way of Allah, and one who did a deed whose like is continued upon him, and a man who gave a charity whose reward is for him while it flows, and a man who left a righteous child supplicating for him.""**"
"4759","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Prophet ﷺ likened the guide to good to its doer, and described Allah's love for aiding the distressed. > **""The guide to good is like its doer, and Allah loves the aiding of the distressed.""**"
"4760","Sahl ibn Sad al-Saidi (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the guide to good to its doer. > **""The guide to good is like its doer.""**"
"4761","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a wife giving charity from her husband's house without his permission. > **""A woman should not give charity from her husband's house except with his permission.""**"
"4762","Umm Sad said she entered upon Aishah (رضي الله عنها) and asked whether a wife giving from her husband's house earns reward for her or for him. Aishah replied it is between them, as told by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, in agreement with the Sahih wording that when she spends without corrupting, he has his reward and she has the like thereof. > **""It is between them. When a woman spends from her husband's house without corrupting, he has his reward and she has the like thereof.""**"
"4763","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised the rank of freeing a neck for leading a blind man forty cubits. > **""Whoever leads a blind man forty cubits has like the freeing of a neck.""**"
"4764","Ibn Umar (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised Paradise for leading a blind man forty steps. > **""Whoever leads a blind man forty steps, Paradise becomes due for him.""**"
"4765","Ibn Abbas (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised forgiveness of forty major sins, including four that entail the Fire, for leading a blind man until bringing him to safety. > **""Whoever leads a blind man until he brings him to safety, forty major sins are forgiven for him, and four majors that entail the Fire.""**"
"4766","Uqbah ibn Amir (رضي الله عنه) reported that a boy came to the Prophet ﷺ, in Musa's wording asking whether he may give his deceased mother's jewelry in charity for her. When he said his mother had not commanded it, the Prophet ﷺ ordered retention. In al-Tabarani's wording the mother died without a will, and the command was to withhold wealth. > **""Did your mother command you that? Then keep your mother's jewelry upon you.""**"
"4767","Anas (رضي الله عنه) reported that Sad (رضي الله عنه) came to the Prophet ﷺ asking whether charity for his mother who died without a will would benefit her. The Prophet ﷺ affirmed and enjoined water. > **""Yes, and upon you is water.""**"
"4768","Sahl ibn Ubadah (رضي الله عنه) said he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asking whether charity for his mother who died without will or charity would be accepted and benefit her. The Prophet ﷺ affirmed even with the least. > **""Yes, even with a burnt hoof of a sheep.""**"
"4769","Abdullah ibn Amr (رضي الله عنهما) reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed one giving voluntary charity to dedicate it to his parents, with full reward for them and no loss to his own. > **""When one gives a voluntary charity, let him make it for his parents; they have its reward, and nothing of his reward is diminished.""**"
"4770","Anas ibn Malik (رضي الله عنه) said he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that when a household gives charity after their dead, Jibril (عليه السلام) bears it on a plate of light, stands at the edge of the grave, and addresses its dweller as one of the deep grave, asking him to accept his family's gift. It enters upon him with joy and glad tidings, while his neighbours to whom nothing is gifted grieve. > **""There is no household among whom one dies, then they give charity for him after his death, but Jibril brings it to him upon a plate of light. Then he stands at the edge of the grave and says: O dweller of the deep grave, this is a gift your family sent to you, so accept it. Then it enters upon him, and he rejoices therein and is gladdened, while his neighbours to whom nothing is gifted grieve.""**"
